var tafsirData_19 = {
  "1": "\"Kaf-Ha-Ya-Ayn-Sad\" opens Surah Maryam with a set of disconnected letters, known in Arabic as the \"huruf muqattaat.\" These letters appear at the beginning of several chapters of the Quran, and their exact meaning is known best to God. They are part of the revelation and should be believed in as they have come.\n\nThe classical scholars offered several explanations. Some said these letters are names of the chapters. Others said they are oaths by which God swears. Others said they point to the miracle of the Quran, which is composed of the very letters the Arabs used in their speech, yet they could not produce anything like it. The Quran is made of these same letters, but its arrangement and meaning are beyond human capacity.\n\nWhat is certain is that these letters serve to draw the listener's attention. After these letters, the chapter immediately begins with the story of God's mercy to Zachariah, the birth of John, the miraculous birth of Jesus, and the stories of Abraham, Moses, Ishmael, and Idris. The opening letters signal that what follows is weighty and that the listener should prepare to receive it.\n\nSome of the early scholars said that these letters contain references to the names of God or to divine attributes. For example, some of the letters appear as roots of words such as \"the Most Merciful\" or \"the Trustworthy.\" But these interpretations, while possible, do not exhaust the meaning.\n\nFor the reader, the best approach is the approach of the early Muslims: believe in them as they have come, without trying to force a single interpretation. The Quran is the word of God, and it contains depths that human understanding may not fully reach.\n\nThe letters also remind us that the Quran is in clear Arabic. God chose a human language to communicate His guidance, and yet that language cannot be matched when used by Him. The very building blocks of Arabic speech are placed at the opening of the chapter as a challenge and as a sign.",
  "2": "“˹This is˺ a reminder of your Lord’s mercy to His servant Zachariah,” opens the account of Zechariah, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the reminder: “This is a reminder of your Lord’s mercy to His servant Zachariah,” or “thikru rahmati rabbika ‘abdahu Zakariyya,” and the commentators note the framing, for the story is presented as the “thikr,” the reminder of the mercy, and the mercy is the “rahmat,” the gift that the story displays, and the servant is the “abd,” the honoured title of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the framing of the story as the mercy is the key, the declaration that the whole account is the display of the divine grace. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mercy is the gift, and the servant is the honour, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of the prayer and the answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mercy, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the stories of the prophets are the reminders of the mercy, and the one who reads the reminder is the one whose hope the mercy sustains, and the believer who trusts the grace is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the framing of the story is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the theme, and the believer who reads the opening is the believer whose hope the mercy sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the frame of the story: the mercy is the theme, and the believer who reads the story with the hope is the believer whose prayer the Lord answers. The verse is the record of the opening, and the teaching of the mercy that the story displays.",
  "3": "“when he cried out to his Lord privately,” records the prayer, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the prayer: “when he cried out to his Lord privately,” or “ith nada rabbahu nida’an khafiyyan,” and the commentators note the prayer, for Zechariah called his Lord with the hidden call, and the word “khafiyyan” — the private, the concealed — is the description, and the commentators note the significance, for the hidden prayer is the sincerity, the supplication that the servant offers in the solitude, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the teaching that the secret call is the dearest, and the commentators connect the verse to the etiquette of the supplication, the declaration that the private prayer is the sincere. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the hidden call is the sincerity, and the sincerity is the key, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the sincerity that the following verses display.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sincerity. The believer is taught that the secret prayer is the sincere, and the one who calls the Lord in the solitude is the one whose call the Lord hears, and the believer who prays in the hidden is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the prayer of Zechariah is the model, the private call that the believer must offer, and the believer who reads the prayer is the believer whose sincerity the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the sincerity of the supplication: Zechariah called in the private, and the believer who prays in the hidden is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the private call is the teaching that the sincerity is the dearest. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the sincerity that the supplication requires.",
  "4": "“saying, “My Lord! Surely my bones have become brittle, and grey hair has spread across my head, but I have never been disappointed in my prayer to You, my Lord!”” records the first part of the supplication, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the supplication: “My Lord! Surely my bones have become brittle, and grey hair has spread across my head, but I have never been disappointed in my prayer to You, my Lord!” or “qala rabbi innee wahana al-‘azmu minnee washta‘ala ar-ra’su shayban wa lam akun bi-du‘a’ika rabbi shaqiyyan,” and the commentators note the supplication, for Zechariah described the weakness of the age and the gratitude of the answer, and the two parts are the plea and the praise, the acknowledgment of the weakness and the remembrance of the favour, and the verse is the record of the supplication, the humility that the following verses complete. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the brittleness of the bones is the age, and the grey hair is the sign, and the remembrance of the answer is the hope, and the verse is the record of the supplication, the plea of the old prophet.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the supplication, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own prayer. The believer is taught that the supplication is the plea and the praise, and the one who acknowledges the weakness and remembers the favour is the one whose prayer the Lord hears, and the believer who prays with the humility is the believer whose prayer the Lord values, and the supplication of Zechariah is the model, the plea of the old and the hope of the answered, and the believer who reads the supplication is the believer whose humility the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the manner of the prayer: Zechariah pleaded and praised, and the believer who prays with the humility and the hope is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the supplication is the teaching that the weakness is the occasion of the plea. The verse is the record of the supplication, and the teaching of the humility that the prayer requires.",
  "5": "“And I am concerned about ˹the faith of˺ my relatives after me, since my wife is barren. So grant me, by Your grace, an heir,” records the concern and the request, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the concern: “And I am concerned about the faith of my relatives after me, since my wife is barren. So grant me, by Your grace, an heir,” or “wa innee khiftu al-mawaliya min wara’ee wa kanati imra’atee ‘aqiran fa-hab lee min ladunka waliyyan,” and the commentators note the concern, for Zechariah feared for the faith of the relatives after him, and the barrenness of the wife, and the request of the heir, and the verse is the record of the concern and the request, the plea that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the concern of Zechariah is the concern of the prophet, the fear that the message would not be carried, and the request of the heir is the request of the continuity, and the verse is the record of the plea, the concern of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the concern is the fear of the faith, and the request is the heir, and the verse is the record of the plea, the concern that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the concern, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own care. The believer is taught that the concern for the faith of the next generation is the concern of the believer, and the one who fears for the message is the one whose fear the Lord values, and the believer who prays for the continuity is the believer whose prayer the Lord hears, and the concern of Zechariah is the model, the fear of the prophet for the faith, and the believer who reads the concern is the believer whose care the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the care of the prophet: Zechariah feared for the faith, and the believer who cares for the faith is the believer whose care the Lord values, and the request of the heir is the teaching that the continuity is the concern of the believer. The verse is the record of the concern, and the teaching of the care that the faith requires.",
  "6": "“who will inherit ˹prophethood˺ from me and the family of Jacob, and make him, O Lord, pleasing ˹to You˺!”” completes the request, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the request: “who will inherit prophethood from me and the family of Jacob, and make him, O Lord, pleasing to You!” or “yarithunee wa yarithu min ali Ya‘qooba waj‘alhu rabbi radiyyan,” and the commentators note the request, for Zechariah asked the heir who would inherit from him and from the family of Jacob, and the request is the continuity, the seeking of the successor, and the closing request is the acceptance, the seeking of the pleasure, and the verse is the record of the completion, the request that the following verse answers with the good news, and the commentators note the significance, for the inheritance is the prophethood and the knowledge, and the pleasure is the end, and the verse is the record of the request, the plea of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the inheritance is the knowledge and the prophethood, and the pleasure is the acceptance, and the verse is the record of the completion, the request that the Lord answered.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the request of the successor is the request of the continuity, and the one who seeks the pleasure of the Lord is the one whose request the Lord grants, and the believer who prays for the acceptance is the believer whose prayer the Lord hears, and the request of Zechariah is the model, the plea for the heir and the pleasure, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose hope the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the request of the continuity: Zechariah asked the heir and the pleasure, and the believer who prays for the faith of the next generation is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the request is the teaching that the pleasure of the Lord is the end. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the request carries.",
  "7": "“˹The angels announced,˺ “O Zachariah! Indeed, We give you the good news of ˹the birth of˺ a son, whose name will be John—a name We have not given to anyone before.”” records the good news, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the good news: “O Zachariah! Indeed, We give you the good news of the birth of a son, whose name will be John—a name We have not given to anyone before,” or “ya Zakariyya inna nubashshiruka bi-ghulamin ismuhu Yahya lam naj‘al lahu min qablu samiyyan,” and the commentators note the good news, for the Lord announced the son, and the name is John, and the name is the new, the one that no one had been given before, and the verse is the record of the good news, the answer to the prayer, and the commentators note the significance, for the naming of the son by the Lord is the honour, and the newness of the name is the sign, and the verse is the record of the good news, the answer that the following verse receives with the wonder. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the name is the gift, and the newness is the sign, and the verse is the record of the good news, the answer of the prayer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer of the believer is the thing that the Lord answers, and the one who calls the Lord is the one whom the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the answer is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the good news of Zechariah is the teaching, the declaration that the prayer is the answered, and the believer who reads the good news is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the prayer: Zechariah was given the son, and the believer who prays is the believer whose prayer the Lord answers, and the name of John is the teaching that the gift of the Lord is the new. The verse is the record of the good news, and the teaching of the hope that the answer carries.",
  "8": "“He wondered, “My Lord! How can I have a son when my wife is barren, and I have become extremely old?”” records the wonder, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the wonder: “My Lord! How can I have a son when my wife is barren, and I have become extremely old?” or “qala rabbi anna yakoonu lee ghulamun wa kanati imra’atee ‘aqiran wa qad balaghtu mina al-kibari ‘itiyyan,” and the commentators note the wonder, for Zechariah wondered at the son in the old age, and the wonder is the human, the astonishment of the reason before the decree, and the verse is the record of the wonder, the astonishment that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the wonder of Zechariah is the wonder of the faithful, the question of the reason, not the doubt of the heart, and the verse is the record of the wonder, the question that the answer would resolve. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the barrenness is the obstacle, and the age is the obstacle, and the verse is the record of the wonder, the question of the two obstacles.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the wonder, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own astonishment. The believer is taught that the wonder of the reason is the human, and the one who questions the decree is the one whose question the Lord answers, and the believer who wonders with the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the wonder of Zechariah is the model, the question of the faithful, and the believer who reads the wonder is the believer whose faith the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the question of the faithful: Zechariah wondered and was answered, and the believer who questions the decree with the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the wonder is the teaching that the decree is the thing that the reason cannot bind. The verse is the record of the wonder, and the teaching of the faith that the answer confirms.",
  "9": "“An angel replied, “So will it be! Your Lord says, ‘It is easy for Me, just as I created you before, when you were nothing!’”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the answer: “So will it be! Your Lord says, ‘It is easy for Me, just as I created you before, when you were nothing!’” or “qala kathalika qala rabbuka huwa ‘alayya hayyinun wa qad khalaqtuka min qablu wa lam taku shay’an,” and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord declared the ease of the matter, and the answer is the teaching, the declaration that the creation of the son is the easy, and the reminder of the first creation is the argument, and the verse is the record of the answer, the correction of the wonder, and the commentators note the significance, for the ease is the attribute, and the reminder of the creation is the proof, and the verse is the record of the answer, the teaching that the power of the Lord is the complete. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ease is the power, and the reminder is the proof, and the verse is the record of the answer, the correction that the following verse applies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ease, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the decree of the Lord is the easy, and the one who measures the power by the obstacles is the one whose measure the answer corrects, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the answer of the Lord is the teaching, the declaration that the creation is the easy, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the ease of the decree: the Lord declared the easy, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the reminder of the first creation is the teaching that the power is the complete. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the trust that the power requires.",
  "10": "“Zachariah said, “My Lord! Grant me a sign.” He responded, “Your sign is that you will not ˹be able to˺ speak to people for three nights, despite being healthy.”” records the request of the sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the request: “My Lord! Grant me a sign. He responded, ‘Your sign is that you will not be able to speak to people for three nights, despite being healthy,’” or “qala rabbi ij‘al lee ayatan qala ayatuka alla tukallima an-nasa thalatha layalin sawiyyan,” and the commentators note the request, for Zechariah asked the sign, and the sign is the silence, the three nights without the speech, and the verse is the record of the request and the answer, the sign that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the sign of the silence is the confirmation, the declaration that the prayer was answered, and the verse is the record of the request, the sign that the Lord granted. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the silence, and the silence is the confirmation, and the verse is the record of the request, the sign that the Lord granted.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the sign of the Lord is the confirmation, and the one who seeks the certainty is the one whom the Lord grants, and the believer who asks the sign is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the sign of Zechariah is the teaching, the declaration that the silence is the confirmation, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confirmation of the sign: Zechariah was given the silence, and the believer who seeks the certainty is the believer whom the Lord confirms, and the sign is the teaching that the prayer is the answered. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the certainty that the sign confirms.",
  "11": "“So he came out to his people from the sanctuary, signalling to them to glorify ˹Allah˺ morning and evening.”” records the emergence, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the emergence: “So he came out to his people from the sanctuary, signalling to them to glorify Allah morning and evening,” or “fa-kharaja ‘ala qawmihi mina al-mihrabi fa-awha ilayhim an sabbihu bukratan wa ‘ashiyyan,” and the commentators note the emergence, for Zechariah came out to the people and signalled the glorification, and the signalling is the gesture, the command of the praise that the silence permitted, and the verse is the record of the emergence, the sign that the following verses develop, and the commentators note the significance, for the emergence is the display of the sign, and the signalling is the command of the praise, and the verse is the record of the emergence, the display of the answer. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the emergence is the display, and the signalling is the command, and the verse is the record of the emergence, the sign that the people saw.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the emergence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own praise. The believer is taught that the sign of the Lord is the occasion of the praise, and the one who receives the sign is the one whose praise the Lord values, and the believer who glorifies the Lord morning and evening is the believer whose praise the Lord accepts, and the emergence of Zechariah is the model, the display of the sign and the command of the praise, and the believer who reads the emergence is the believer whose praise the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the praise of the sign: Zechariah signalled the glorification, and the believer who receives the sign is the believer whose praise the Lord values, and the emergence is the teaching that the sign is the occasion of the praise. The verse is the record of the emergence, and the teaching of the praise that the sign invites.",
  "12": "“˹It was later said,˺ “O John! Hold firmly to the Scriptures.” And We granted him wisdom while ˹he was still˺ a child,” records the command to John, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: “O John! Hold firmly to the Scriptures. And We granted him wisdom while he was still a child,” or “ya Yahya khuthi al-kitaba bi-quwwatin wa ataynahu al-hukma sabiyyan,” and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded John to hold the Scripture firmly, and the command is the charge, the duty of the prophet, and the verse then records the wisdom, the grant that the Lord gave in the childhood, and the verse is the record of the command and the gift, the charge and the wisdom that the following verses describe, and the commentators note the significance, for the wisdom in the childhood is the wonder, the grant of the judgment to the young, and the verse is the record of the command, the charge that the prophet received. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the holding of the Scripture is the duty, and the wisdom is the gift, and the verse is the record of the command, the charge and the gift of John.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the charge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the holding of the Scripture is the duty, and the one who holds it firmly is the one whose holding the Lord values, and the believer who takes the Book with the strength is the believer whose duty the Lord accepts, and the command to John is the model, the charge of the prophet, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose duty the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the charge of the Scripture: John was commanded to hold it, and the believer who takes the Book firmly is the believer whose duty the Lord values, and the wisdom in the childhood is the teaching that the gift of the Lord is the wonder. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the duty that the Scripture imposes.",
  "13": "“as well as purity and compassion from Us. And he was God-fearing,” continues the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the description: “as well as purity and compassion from Us. And he was God-fearing,” or “wa hananan min ladunna wa zakatan wa kana taqiyyan,” and the commentators note the description, for the Lord granted John the compassion and the purity, and the description is the praise, the declaration that John was the God-fearing, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the compassion is the tenderness, and the purity is the cleansing, and the taqwa is the fear, and the three together are the portrait of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the compassion is the gift, and the purity is the gift, and the taqwa is the state, and the verse is the record of the description, the portrait of John.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the description, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own character. The believer is taught that the compassion and the purity and the fear are the qualities of the prophet, and the one who cultivates the three is the one whose character the Lord values, and the believer who seeks the taqwa is the believer whose fear the Lord accepts, and the description of John is the model, the portrait that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose character the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the qualities of the prophet: John was the compassionate and the pure and the fearing, and the believer who cultivates the three is the believer whose character the Lord values, and the description is the teaching that the taqwa is the crown. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the character that the prophet models.",
  "14": "“and kind to his parents. He was neither arrogant nor disobedient.”” completes the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the description: “and kind to his parents. He was neither arrogant nor disobedient,” or “wa barran bi-walidayhi wa lam yakun jabbaran ‘asiyyan,” and the commentators note the description, for John was the kind to the parents, and the verse then negates the two faults, the arrogance and the disobedience, and the verse is the record of the description, the completion of the portrait, and the commentators note the significance, for the kindness to the parents is the first of the duties, and the negation of the arrogance and the disobedience is the freedom from the faults, and the verse is the record of the completion, the portrait of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the kindness is the duty, and the negation is the purity, and the verse is the record of the completion, the portrait of John.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the kindness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the kindness to the parents is the duty, and the one who honours the parents is the one whose duty the Lord values, and the believer who avoids the arrogance is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the description of John is the model, the portrait that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose character the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the parents: John was the kind, and the believer who honours the parents is the believer whose duty the Lord values, and the negation of the arrogance is the teaching that the humility is the mark of the prophet. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the kindness that the prophet models.",
  "15": "“Peace be upon him the day he was born, and the day of his death, and the day he will be raised back to life!”” records the peace, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the peace: “Peace be upon him the day he was born, and the day of his death, and the day he will be raised back to life!” or “wa salamun ‘alayhi yawma wulida wa yawma yamootu wa yawma yub‘athu hayyan,” and the commentators note the peace, for the Lord granted John the peace in the three days, and the three days are the whole life, the birth and the death and the resurrection, and the verse is the record of the peace, the closing of the account, and the commentators note the significance, for the peace in the three days is the complete safety, and the verse is the record of the peace, the blessing that the story closes with. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the three days are the stations, and the peace is the safety, and the verse is the record of the peace, the blessing of John.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the peace, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the peace of the Lord is the thing that the believer seeks, and the one who lives in the obedience is the one whom the peace covers, and the believer who hopes the safety is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace of John is the teaching, the declaration that the three stations are the covered, and the believer who reads the peace is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the peace: John was granted the peace in the three days, and the believer who lives in the obedience is the believer whom the peace covers, and the peace is the blessing that the believer seeks. The verse is the record of the peace, and the teaching of the hope that the peace carries.",
  "16": "“And mention in the Book ˹O Prophet, the story of˺ Mary when she withdrew from her family to a place in the east,” opens the story of Mary, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse commands the mention: “And mention in the Book O Prophet, the story of Mary when she withdrew from her family to a place in the east,” or “wathkur fi al-kitabi Maryama ithi intabathat min ahliha makanan sharqiyyan,” and the commentators note the command, for the Prophet was commanded to mention Mary, and the withdrawal is the seclusion, the retreat of Mary to the eastern place, and the verse is the record of the command, the opening of the story that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the withdrawal is the seclusion of the worship, and the eastern place is the solitude, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of the pure. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the withdrawal is the seclusion, and the seclusion is the worship, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of Mary.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the seclusion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own retreat. The believer is taught that the seclusion is the worship, and the one who withdraws for the devotion is the one whose devotion the Lord values, and the believer who retreats for the prayer is the believer whose retreat the Lord accepts, and the withdrawal of Mary is the model, the seclusion of the pure, and the believer who reads the opening is the believer whose devotion the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seclusion of the worship: Mary withdrew for the devotion, and the believer who retreats for the prayer is the believer whose devotion the Lord values, and the withdrawal is the teaching that the solitude is the worship. The verse is the record of the opening, and the teaching of the seclusion that the devotion requires.",
  "17": "“screening herself off from them. Then We sent to her Our angel, ˹Gabriel,˺ appearing before her as a man, perfectly formed.”” records the screening and the angel, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the screening: “screening herself off from them. Then We sent to her Our angel, Gabriel, appearing before her as a man, perfectly formed,” or “fattakhathat min doonihim hijaban fa-arsalna ilayha roohana fa-tamaththala laha basharan sawiyyan,” and the commentators note the screening, for Mary screened herself, and the sending of the angel, and the appearance of the angel as the perfect man, and the verse is the record of the screening and the sending, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the screen is the veil, and the angel is the Gabriel, and the appearance is the form of the man, and the verse is the record of the sending, the visit that the following verse answers with the fear. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the screen is the veil, and the angel is the Gabriel, and the verse is the record of the sending, the visit of the angel.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sending, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own purity. The believer is taught that the purity of Mary is the thing that the Lord honoured, and the one who guards the chastity is the one whom the Lord visits, and the believer who keeps the purity is the believer whose purity the Lord values, and the sending of the angel is the teaching, the declaration that the pure is the honoured, and the believer who reads the sending is the believer whose purity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honour of the purity: Mary was visited by the angel, and the believer who keeps the purity is the believer whom the Lord honours, and the sending is the teaching that the chastity is the thing that the Lord values. The verse is the record of the sending, and the teaching of the purity that the angel honoured.",
  "18": "“She appealed, “I truly seek refuge in the Most Compassionate from you! ˹So leave me alone˺ if you are God-fearing.”” records the appeal, and the commentators note the two parts of the appeal. The verse records the appeal: “I truly seek refuge in the Most Compassionate from you! So leave me alone if you are God-fearing,” or “qalat innee a‘oothu bir-rahmani minka in kunta taqiyyan,” and the commentators note the appeal, for Mary sought the refuge in the Most Compassionate, and the appeal is the chastity, the seeking of the protection, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the purity that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the refuge of Mary is the model of the chaste, and the appeal to the Most Compassionate is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the purity of the pure. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the refuge is the protection, and the appeal is the chastity, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the purity that the angel answered.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the refuge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own chastity. The believer is taught that the refuge in the Lord is the shield, and the one who seeks the protection is the one whom the Lord protects, and the believer who appeals to the Most Compassionate is the believer whose chastity the Lord values, and the appeal of Mary is the model, the refuge of the pure, and the believer who reads the appeal is the believer whose chastity the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the refuge of the chaste: Mary sought the protection, and the believer who seeks the refuge is the believer whom the Lord protects, and the appeal is the teaching that the chastity is the shield. The verse is the record of the appeal, and the teaching of the refuge that the purity requires.",
  "19": "“He responded, “I am only a messenger from your Lord, ˹sent˺ to bless you with a pure son.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “I am only a messenger from your Lord, sent to bless you with a pure son,” or “qala innama ana rasoolu rabbiki li-ahaba laki ghulaman zakiyyan,” and the commentators note the reply, for the angel declared the mission, and the reply is the reassurance, the declaration that the angel is the messenger and the gift is the son, and the verse is the record of the reply, the reassurance that the following verse answers with the wonder, and the commentators note the significance, for the reply is the declaration of the good news, and the word \"ghulam zakiyy\" — the pure son — is the description of Jesus, and the verse is the record of the reply, the reassurance of the pure. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the messenger is the angel, and the son is the gift, and the verse is the record of the reply, the reassurance that the following verse receives.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reassurance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the word of the Lord is the reassurance, and the one who receives the word is the one whose fear the word calms, and the believer who trusts the message is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the reply of the angel is the model, the reassurance that the believer receives, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose trust the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reassurance of the Lord: the angel declared the mission, and the believer who receives the word is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the reply is the teaching that the message is the mercy. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the trust that the message produces.",
  "20": "“She wondered, “How can I have a son when no man has ever touched me, nor am I unchaste?”” records the wonder, and the commentators note the two parts of the wonder. The verse records the wonder: “How can I have a son when no man has ever touched me, nor am I unchaste?” or “qalat anna yakoonu lee ghulamun wa lam yamsasnee basharun wa lam aku baghiyyan,” and the commentators note the wonder, for Mary wondered at the son without the touch of the man, and the wonder is the chastity, the declaration of the purity, and the verse is the record of the wonder, the chastity that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the wonder of Mary is the statement of the purity, the declaration that the birth would be the miracle, and the verse is the record of the wonder, the chastity of the pure. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the wonder is the chastity, and the chastity is the purity, and the verse is the record of the wonder, the chastity that the answer would confirm.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the wonder, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own chastity. The believer is taught that the chastity is the thing that the Lord honours, and the one who declares the purity is the one whose purity the Lord confirms, and the believer who guards the chastity is the believer whose purity the Lord values, and the wonder of Mary is the model, the declaration of the pure, and the believer who reads the wonder is the believer whose chastity the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the statement of the purity: Mary declared the chastity, and the believer who guards the purity is the believer whose purity the Lord confirms, and the wonder is the teaching that the chastity is the shield. The verse is the record of the wonder, and the teaching of the chastity that the birth honoured.",
  "21": "“He replied, “So will it be! Your Lord says, ‘It is easy for Me. And so will We make him a sign for humanity and a mercy from Us.’ It is a matter ˹already˺ decreed.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: “So will it be! Your Lord says, ‘It is easy for Me. And so will We make him a sign for humanity and a mercy from Us.’ It is a matter already decreed,” or “qala kathaliki qala rabbuki huwa ‘alayya hayyinun wa li-naj‘alahu ayatan lin-nasi wa rahmatan minna wa kana amran maqdiyyan,” and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord declared the ease of the matter, and the answer is the teaching, the declaration that the birth without the father is the easy, and the purpose is the sign and the mercy, and the closing is the decree, and the verse is the record of the answer, the correction of the wonder, and the commentators note the significance, for the ease is the power, and the sign is the purpose, and the decree is the certainty, and the verse is the record of the answer, the teaching that the power of the Lord is the complete. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ease is the power, and the sign is the purpose, and the verse is the record of the answer, the correction that the following verse fulfills.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ease, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the decree of the Lord is the easy, and the one who measures the power by the obstacles is the one whose measure the answer corrects, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the answer of the Lord is the teaching, the declaration that the creation is the easy, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the ease of the decree: the Lord declared the easy, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the sign and the mercy are the teaching that the gift of the Lord is the purpose. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the trust that the power requires.",
  "22": "“So she conceived him and withdrew with him to a remote place.”” records the conception and the withdrawal, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the conception: “So she conceived him and withdrew with him to a remote place,” or “fa-hamalathu fantabathat bihi makanan qasiyyan,” and the commentators note the conception, for Mary conceived the son, and the withdrawal to the remote place, and the verse is the record of the conception and the withdrawal, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the conception is the miracle, and the withdrawal is the seclusion, and the verse is the record of the conception, the beginning of the birth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the conception is the miracle, and the withdrawal is the seclusion, and the verse is the record of the conception, the step that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the conception, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the conception of the miracle is the thing that the Lord decrees, and the one who bears the trial is the one whom the Lord supports, and the believer who trusts the decree is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the conception of Mary is the teaching, the declaration that the decree is the certain, and the believer who reads the conception is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the bearing of the decree: Mary conceived and withdrew, and the believer who bears the trial is the believer whom the Lord supports, and the conception is the teaching that the decree is the thing that the believer bears with the trust. The verse is the record of the conception, and the teaching of the trust that the decree requires.",
  "23": "“Then the pains of labour drove her to the trunk of a palm tree. She cried, “Alas! I wish I had died before this, and was a thing long forgotten!”” records the labour, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the labour: “Then the pains of labour drove her to the trunk of a palm tree. She cried, ‘Alas! I wish I had died before this, and was a thing long forgotten!’” or “fa-aja’aha al-makhadu ila jith‘i an-nakhlati qalat ya laytanee mittu qabla hatha wa kuntu nasyan mansiyyan,” and the commentators note the labour, for the pains drove Mary to the trunk of the palm, and the cry is the human, the grief of the trial, and the verse is the record of the labour, the human sorrow that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the cry of Mary is the human, the sorrow of the one who bore the trial alone, and the verse is the record of the labour, the grief that the reassurance would lift. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the palm is the support, and the cry is the human, and the verse is the record of the labour, the grief that the following verse would heal.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sorrow, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trial. The believer is taught that the sorrow of the trial is the human, and the one who bears the grief is the one whom the Lord comforts, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the pain is the believer whose pain the Lord heals, and the cry of Mary is the teaching, the declaration that the human grief is the thing that the Lord sees, and the believer who reads the cry is the believer whose hope the reassurance sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the sorrow: Mary cried and was comforted, and the believer who bears the grief is the believer whom the Lord comforts, and the cry is the teaching that the trial is the human, and the comfort is the divine. The verse is the record of the labour, and the teaching of the comfort that the grief receives.",
  "24": "“So a voice reassured her from below her, “Do not grieve! Your Lord has provided a stream at your feet.”” records the reassurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the reassurance: “So a voice reassured her from below her, ‘Do not grieve! Your Lord has provided a stream at your feet,’” or “fa-nadaha min tahtiha alla tahzanee qad ja‘ala rabbuki tahtaki sariyyan,” and the commentators note the reassurance, for the voice called Mary from below, and the call is the comfort, the declaration that the grief is the lifted, and the stream is the provision, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the voice is the sign, and the stream is the provision, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the voice is the call, and the stream is the provision, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reassurance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own comfort. The believer is taught that the reassurance of the Lord is the comfort, and the one who grieves is the one whom the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the grief is the believer whose grief the Lord lifts, and the reassurance of Mary is the teaching, the declaration that the Lord provides at the moment of the need, and the believer who reads the reassurance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the comfort of the Lord: Mary was reassured and provided, and the believer who grieves is the believer whom the Lord comforts, and the stream is the teaching that the provision of the Lord is the near. The verse is the record of the reassurance, and the teaching of the comfort that the grief receives.",
  "25": "“And shake the trunk of this palm tree towards you, it will drop fresh, ripe dates upon you.”” records the command of the palm, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: “And shake the trunk of this palm tree towards you, it will drop fresh, ripe dates upon you,” or “wa huzzee ilayki bi-jith‘i an-nakhlati tusaqit ‘alayki rutaban janiyyan,” and the commentators note the command, for the voice commanded Mary to shake the palm, and the command is the provision, the teaching that the means is the thing that the believer uses, and the dates are the gift, and the verse is the record of the command, the provision that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the shaking of the palm is the means, and the dates are the gift, and the verse is the record of the command, the teaching that the believer takes the means and trusts the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the shaking is the means, and the dates are the provision, and the verse is the record of the command, the provision that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the means, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the means is the duty, and the one who takes the means is the one whose provision the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord while taking the means is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the command of the palm is the teaching, the declaration that the means and the trust are the two companions, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose reliance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the balance of the means: Mary shook the palm and the dates fell, and the believer who takes the means and trusts the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord accepts, and the command is the teaching that the means is the duty. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the reliance that the provision requires.",
  "26": "“So eat and drink, and put your heart at ease. But if you see any of the people, say, ‘I have vowed silence to the Most Compassionate, so I am not talking to anyone today.’”” completes the reassurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the reassurance: “So eat and drink, and put your heart at ease. But if you see any of the people, say, ‘I have vowed silence to the Most Compassionate, so I am not talking to anyone today,’” or “fa-kulee washrabee wa qarree ‘aynan fa-imma tarayinna mina al-bashari ahadan fa-qoolee innee nathartu lir-rahmani sawman fa-lan ukallima al-yawma insiyyan,” and the commentators note the completion, for the voice commanded the eating and the drinking and the ease, and the vow of the silence, and the verse is the record of the completion, the provision and the instruction that the following verses develop, and the commentators note the significance, for the vow of the silence is the instruction, the means by which the infant would speak, and the verse is the record of the completion, the instruction of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ease is the comfort, and the vow is the instruction, and the verse is the record of the completion, the instruction that the following verse displays.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the instruction, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the instruction of the Lord is the guidance, and the one who follows the instruction is the one whose affair the Lord arranges, and the believer who trusts the guidance is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the instruction of Mary is the teaching, the declaration that the Lord arranges the defense, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the guidance of the Lord: Mary was instructed the silence, and the believer who follows the guidance is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the vow is the teaching that the Lord is the arranger of the defense. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the guidance requires.",
  "27": "“Then she returned to her people, carrying him. They said ˹in shock˺, “O Mary! You have certainly done a horrible thing!”” records the return and the accusation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the return: “Then she returned to her people, carrying him. They said in shock, ‘O Mary! You have certainly done a horrible thing!’” or “fa-atat bihi qawmaha tahmiluhu qaloo ya Maryamu laqad ji’ti shay’an fariyyan,” and the commentators note the return, for Mary returned to the people carrying the infant, and the accusation is the shock, the charge of the people, and the verse is the record of the return, the accusation that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the accusation is the slander, and the slander is the thing that the speech of the infant would answer, and the verse is the record of the return, the accusation of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the return is the display, and the accusation is the charge, and the verse is the record of the return, the accusation that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the accusation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the accusation of the falsehood is the trial, and the one who bears the slander is the one whose truth the Lord establishes, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the accusation is the believer whose innocence the Lord exposes, and the accusation of Mary is the teaching, the declaration that the falsehood charges the pure, and the believer who reads the return is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the accused: Mary was charged and vindicated, and the believer who bears the slander is the believer whose truth the Lord establishes, and the accusation is the teaching that the pure is the accused. The verse is the record of the return, and the teaching of the patience that the accusation demands.",
  "28": "“O sister of Aaron! Your father was not an indecent man, nor was your mother unchaste.”” completes the accusation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the accusation: “O sister of Aaron! Your father was not an indecent man, nor was your mother unchaste,” or “ya ukhta Haroona ma kana abooki imra’a saw’in wa ma kanat ummuki baghiyyan,” and the commentators note the accusation, for the people addressed Mary as the sister of Aaron, and the mention of the parents is the reproach, the declaration that the purity of the lineage was the thing that the deed had contradicted, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reproach that the following verse answers with the pointing, and the commentators note the significance, for the title \"sister of Aaron\" is the lineage of the purity, and the mention of the parents is the measure of the charge, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reproach of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the title is the lineage, and the reproach is the charge, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reproach that the infant would answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reproach, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reputation. The believer is taught that the reputation of the family is the trust, and the one who is charged is the one whose family the charge touches, and the believer who guards the honour is the believer whose honour the Lord protects, and the reproach of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the falsehood strikes at the pure lineage, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the charge: the people reproached the lineage, and the believer who guards the honour is the believer whose honour the Lord defends, and the reproach is the teaching that the falsehood strikes at the pure. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the honour that the charge attacks.",
  "29": "“So she pointed to the baby. They exclaimed, “How can we talk to someone who is an infant in the cradle?”” records the pointing, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the pointing: “So she pointed to the baby. They exclaimed, ‘How can we talk to someone who is an infant in the cradle?’” or “fa-asharat ilayhi qaloo kayfa nukallimu man kana fi al-mahdi sabiyyan,” and the commentators note the pointing, for Mary pointed to the infant, and the pointing is the obedience, the fulfillment of the vow of the silence, and the exclamation of the people is the wonder, and the verse is the record of the pointing, the step that the following verse answers with the speech, and the commentators note the significance, for the pointing is the instruction, and the exclamation is the occasion, and the verse is the record of the pointing, the step of the vindication. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the pointing is the obedience, and the exclamation is the wonder, and the verse is the record of the pointing, the step that the speech would answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the pointing, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the obedience of the instruction is the reliance, and the one who follows the command is the one whose affair the Lord arranges, and the believer who trusts the arrangement is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the pointing of Mary is the model, the obedience that the vindication followed, and the believer who reads the pointing is the believer whose reliance the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the obedience of the instruction: Mary pointed and the infant spoke, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the pointing is the teaching that the obedience is the reliance. The verse is the record of the pointing, and the teaching of the reliance that the obedience requires.",
  "30": "“Jesus declared, “I am truly a servant of Allah. He has destined me to be given the Scripture and to be a prophet.”” records the speech of the infant, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the speech: “Jesus declared, ‘I am truly a servant of Allah. He has destined me to be given the Scripture and to be a prophet,’” or “qala innee ‘abdu Allahi ataniya al-kitaba wa ja‘alanee nabiyyan,” and the commentators note the speech, for the infant Jesus declared the servitude, and the declaration is the vindication, the first word of the infant, and the verse is the record of the speech, the vindication that the following verses complete, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration of the servitude is the key, the statement that Jesus is the servant of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the speech, the vindication of the mother and the declaration of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the servitude is the truth, and the Scripture and the prophethood are the gifts, and the verse is the record of the speech, the declaration of the infant.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own faith. The believer is taught that the declaration of the servitude is the truth, and the one who declares the servitude is the one whose truth the Lord establishes, and the believer who holds the servitude is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the speech of Jesus is the model, the declaration of the servant, and the believer who reads the speech is the believer whose faith the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the truth of the servitude: Jesus declared the servitude, and the believer who holds the servitude is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the speech is the teaching that the servitude is the foundation. The verse is the record of the speech, and the teaching of the faith that the declaration establishes.",
  "31": "“He has made me a blessing wherever I go, and bid me to establish prayer and give alms-tax as long as I live,” continues the speech, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the speech: “He has made me a blessing wherever I go, and bid me to establish prayer and give alms-tax as long as I live,” or “wa ja‘alanee mubarakan ayna ma kuntu wa awsanee bis-salati waz-zakati ma dumtu hayyan,” and the commentators note the speech, for Jesus declared the blessing and the two commands, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the speech that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the blessing is the benefit, and the prayer and the zakah are the two pillars, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the speech of the infant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the blessing is the benefit, and the two commands are the pillars, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the speech that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the blessing, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worship. The believer is taught that the blessing is the benefit, and the one who is the blessing is the one whose presence the Lord benefits, and the believer who establishes the prayer and gives the zakah is the believer whose worship the Lord values, and the speech of Jesus is the model, the declaration of the blessing and the commands, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose worship the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the marks of the servant: Jesus was the blessing and the worshipper, and the believer who is the benefit and establishes the prayer is the believer whose worship the Lord accepts, and the continuation is the teaching that the worship is the mark of the servant. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the worship that the blessing requires.",
  "32": "“and to be kind to my mother. He has not made me arrogant or defiant.”” continues the speech, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the speech: “and to be kind to my mother. He has not made me arrogant or defiant,” or “wa barran bi-walidatee wa lam yaj‘alnee jabbaran shaqiyyan,” and the commentators note the speech, for Jesus declared the kindness to the mother, and the negation of the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the speech that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the kindness to the mother is the vindication of Mary, and the negation of the arrogance is the purity, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the speech of the infant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the kindness is the vindication, and the negation is the purity, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the speech that the following verse closes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the kindness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the kindness to the mother is the duty, and the one who honours the mother is the one whose duty the Lord values, and the believer who avoids the arrogance is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the speech of Jesus is the model, the declaration of the kindness and the humility, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose character the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the mother: Jesus declared the kindness, and the believer who honours the mother is the believer whose duty the Lord values, and the negation of the arrogance is the teaching that the humility is the mark of the servant. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the kindness that the duty requires.",
  "33": "“Peace be upon me the day I was born, the day I die, and the day I will be raised back to life!”” closes the speech, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse closes the speech: “Peace be upon me the day I was born, the day I die, and the day I will be raised back to life!” or “was-salamu ‘alayya yawma wulidtu wa yawma amootu wa yawma ub‘athu hayyan,” and the commentators note the peace, for Jesus declared the peace in the three days, and the three days are the stations, and the verse is the record of the closing, the peace that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the peace in the three days is the complete safety, and the declaration of the death and the resurrection is the proof that Jesus is the servant, and the verse is the record of the closing, the peace of the infant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the three days are the stations, and the peace is the safety, and the verse is the record of the closing, the peace that the servant declared.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the peace, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the peace of the Lord is the thing that the believer seeks, and the one who lives in the servitude is the one whom the peace covers, and the believer who hopes the safety is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the peace of Jesus is the teaching, the declaration that the servant is the covered, and the believer who reads the closing is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the peace: Jesus declared the peace in the three days, and the believer who lives in the servitude is the believer whom the peace covers, and the closing is the teaching that the servant is the safe. The verse is the record of the closing, and the teaching of the hope that the peace carries.",
  "34": "“That is Jesus, son of Mary. ˹And this is˺ a word of truth, about which they dispute.”” closes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse closes the account: “That is Jesus, son of Mary. And this is a word of truth, about which they dispute,” or “thalika ‘Eesa ibnu Maryama qawla al-haqqi allathee feehi yamtaroon,” and the commentators note the closing, for the verse identifies Jesus as the son of Mary, and the declaration of the word of truth, and the verse is the record of the closing, the correction of the disputes, and the commentators note the significance, for the identification of Jesus as the son of Mary is the correction of the claims, and the word of truth is the declaration that the account is the true, and the dispute is the thing that the truth answers, and the verse is the record of the closing, the correction that the story delivers. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the son of Mary is the identification, and the word of truth is the correction, and the verse is the record of the closing, the correction of the disputes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the truth, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the account of Jesus is the word of truth, and the one who holds the truth is the one whose faith the Lord confirms, and the believer who rejects the disputes is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the closing of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer holds, and the believer who reads the closing is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the truth: the account is the true, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the closing is the teaching that the dispute is the thing that the truth resolves. The verse is the record of the closing, and the teaching of the certainty that the truth produces.",
  "35": "\"It is not for Allah to take a son! Glory be to Him. When He decrees a matter, He simply tells it, 'Be!' And it is!\" makes a definitive statement about God's freedom from having children. The previous verse identified Jesus as the son of Mary. This verse rejects any claim that he is the son of God in a divine sense.\n\n\"It is not for Allah to take a son\" is a strong, almost indignant statement. It means that this is something that does not befit God, something that is beneath His majesty. God does not have offspring. He does not need them. He is the Self-Sufficient, the Eternal, the Creator of everything.\n\nThe word \"Glory be to Him\" is an immediate declaration of transcendence. As soon as the idea of a son is mentioned, God is glorified above it. This is the Quran's way of protecting the divine nature from any limitation. Having a child implies need, dependency, and likeness to creation, all of which are impossible for God.\n\nThe explanation given is simple and powerful: when God decrees something, He says \"Be!\" and it is. He does not need a son to carry on His work. He does not need a family. His will is instantly effective. The entire universe came into being through His word. A being with this kind of power has no need of offspring.\n\nThis argument also applies to the birth of Jesus. God said \"Be\" and Jesus was, without a father. The same power that created Jesus is the power that creates everything. A miracle does not change the nature of the one who performs it or the one through whom it is performed.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear call to pure monotheism. God is far above having children. To claim that He has a son is to misunderstand who He is. His power is complete, His will is absolute, and His nature is utterly unique. Worship Him alone.",
  "36": "\"Jesus also declared, 'Surely Allah is my Lord and your Lord, so worship Him alone. This is the Straight Path'\" gives the core of Jesus's message. The previous verse said God creates by saying \"Be.\" This verse records the teaching of Jesus himself.\n\nJesus says, \"Surely Allah is my Lord and your Lord.\" He places himself on the same level as his hearers before God. He is a servant with a Lord. They are servants with a Lord. The same God who is his Lord is their Lord. There is no distinction between him and them in terms of divinity.\n\nThe command follows: \"so worship Him alone.\" This is the same message delivered by every prophet. Worship is directed to God alone, not to Jesus, not to Mary, not to saints, not to idols. Jesus himself delivers this command, making it impossible to claim that he taught people to worship him.\n\n\"This is the Straight Path\" means that the worship of the one God, without partners, is the path of truth, balance, and salvation. It is straight because it leads directly to God without detours into idolatry, confusion, or exaggeration.\n\nThis verse is particularly important in interfaith discussions. The Quran presents Jesus himself preaching the same monotheism that Muhammad preached. Anyone who claims to follow Jesus should follow his actual teaching, which is to worship God alone.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a summary of the entire religion. Know that God is your Lord. Worship Him alone. Walk the Straight Path. This is what Jesus taught. This is what Muhammad taught. This is what every prophet taught. It is simple, clear, and uncompromising.\n\nThe placement of this teaching in the cradle account also matters. The Quran has Jesus confess servanthood as his first recorded words and preach pure monotheism as his summary, and everything between those two statements is commentary. Any later theology that departs from either end, the verse implies, has departed from what the child himself announced.",
  "37": "\"Yet their various groups have differed among themselves about him, so woe to the disbelievers when they face a tremendous Day!\" laments the divisions that arose after Jesus. The previous verse gave his clear teaching. This verse describes what people did with it.\n\nThe \"various groups\" refers to the different sects and denominations that emerged among the followers of Jesus. Some believed he was God. Some believed he was the son of God. Some believed he was one of three in a trinity. Some believed he was merely a human prophet. The Quran says they differed among themselves, and many of them fell into serious error.\n\nThe phrase \"woe to the disbelievers\" is a warning. Woe is a word of threat and impending punishment. Those who disbelieved in Jesus by rejecting his message, or who disbelieved by exaggerating his status beyond what he taught, will face a tremendous Day.\n\nThat Day is the Day of Judgment, described as \"tremendous\" because of its terrors. On that Day, all disputes will be settled. The groups that argued about Jesus will learn the truth, but it will be too late for those who died in falsehood.\n\nThe verse does not condemn sincere followers of Jesus who tried to follow his message according to the light they had. It condemns those who disbelieved, whether by rejecting the prophets or by distorting their teachings after them.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning against religious division and speculation. When people go beyond the clear teachings of the prophets, they split into sects and lose the truth. Hold to what God has revealed. Do not add to it or subtract from it. The Straight Path is the path of unity in truth.\n\nNote the word 'groups.' The error is not imagined as one people misreading one text, but as a scattering of parties, each with its own version, arguing with the others. Division itself becomes evidence: a clear teaching produces unity, and the straight path is one road, while what has been bent produces as many paths as there are interests to serve.",
  "38": "\"How clearly will they hear and see on the Day they will come to Us! But today the wrongdoers are clearly astray\" describes the clarity of the Day of Judgment in contrast to the blindness of this world. The previous verse warned of a tremendous Day. This verse gives a glimpse of what it will be like.\n\nOn that Day, the disbelievers will hear and see with perfect clarity. The truths they denied will be unmistakable. They will see the prophets, the books, the angels, Paradise, and Hell. They will hear the truth they refused to hear in this world. Their spiritual blindness will be gone, but it will be too late.\n\nThe phrase \"how clearly will they hear and see\" is both an observation and a rebuke. In this life, they had ears but did not hear, eyes but did not see. In the next life, their hearing and sight will be perfect, but the perfection will only increase their regret.\n\n\"But today the wrongdoers are clearly astray\" returns to the present. In this world, they are lost in error. The contrast between the clarity of the Hereafter and the confusion of this world is stark. The same people who cannot see the truth now will see it all too clearly then.\n\nThe verse is a call to use your hearing and sight while they can still benefit you. Listen to the Quran. Look at the signs of God. Understand while there is time. The clarity that comes after death cannot save you. The clarity that comes through faith in this life can.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning not to waste the opportunity of this life. Your ears and eyes are trusts. Use them to seek the truth now, before the Day when every knee will bend and every dispute will be settled.",
  "39": "\"And warn them O Prophet of the Day of Regret, when all matters will be settled, while they are engrossed in heedlessness and disbelief\" commands the Prophet to warn his people. The previous verse described the clarity of the Hereafter. This verse names it as the Day of Regret.\n\nThe Day of Regret, or \"Yawm al-Hasrah,\" is one of the names of the Day of Judgment. It is the day when people will regret everything: every missed prayer, every act of disobedience, every moment of heedlessness. The people of Paradise will regret the time they wasted not increasing in good. The people of Hell will regret the entire lives they spent in disbelief.\n\nOn that Day, all matters will be settled. The disputes will be judged, the deeds weighed, and the destinations assigned. There will be no second chance, no bargaining, no return to this world. The matter is final.\n\nThe people being warned are described as \"engrossed in heedlessness and disbelief.\" They are completely absorbed in the affairs of this world, giving no thought to the next. Their heedlessness is not innocent. It is a deliberate ignoring of the warnings they have received.\n\nThe command to warn is given to the Prophet and, by extension, to every believer who carries the message. Warning is an act of mercy. It gives people the chance to wake up before the Day when waking up is too late.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a wake-up call. Are you engrossed in heedlessness? Are you living as if this world will last forever? The Day of Regret is coming. Settle your matters now, while you still can. Do not let yourself be among those whose greatest regret is that they did not prepare.\n\nThe name of the Day also explains why the warning is paired with the description of people engrossed in heedlessness. Regret is the emotion of realized loss, what a person feels when the window has closed, and it is the one feeling that Day will supply in full. The Prophet's warning exists to pre-empt it: regret felt now, while it can still change something, is called repentance.",
  "40": "\"Indeed, it is We Who will succeed the earth and whoever is on it. And to Us they will all be returned\" states the ultimate ownership of all things. The previous verse warned of the Day when matters will be settled. This verse explains who inherits the earth.\n\nThe word \"succeed\" means to inherit. God is the final inheritor of the earth and everything on it. Every kingdom, every fortune, every monument, and every person will return to God. The owners of this world are temporary tenants. God is the permanent owner.\n\nThis is a humbling truth. The Pharaohs, the Caesars, the emperors, and the billionaires all thought they owned the earth. None of them took it with them. The earth remains, and its inhabitants die, and everything returns to the One who gave it.\n\nThe phrase \"and to Us they will all be returned\" is repeated throughout the Quran. It is the final answer to every claim of ownership, every act of arrogance, and every illusion of permanence. You came from God, and you will return to God. Nothing and no one else has any permanent claim on you.\n\nThe verse also contains comfort for the oppressed. The wrongdoer who seized land, wealth, or power in this world will have to surrender it all. The earth will not remain in unjust hands. Its final owner is the Just King.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder to hold the things of this world lightly. You are a temporary custodian, not a permanent owner. Use what you have for the purposes of the One who will one day take it all back. The best thing you can leave behind is righteous deeds that will benefit you after you return.\n\n'Whoever is on it' includes the verse's first hearers. The Meccans who held the sanctuary, the trade routes, and the town's pride are told that the earth and they themselves will both be inherited by God, and the statement answers pride in its own currency. What a person cannot keep, he cannot finally own.",
  "41": "“And mention in the Book ˹O Prophet, the story of˺ Abraham. He was surely a man of truth and a prophet.”” opens the account of Abraham, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse commands the mention: “And mention in the Book O Prophet, the story of Abraham. He was surely a man of truth and a prophet,” or “wathkur fi al-kitabi Ibraheema innahu kana siddeeqan nabiyyan,” and the commentators note the command, for the Prophet was commanded to mention Abraham, and the description is the praise, the “siddeeq,” the man of the truth, and the “nabiyy,” the prophet, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the title of the \"siddeeq\" is the highest, the declaration that Abraham was the man of the complete truth, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of the friend of God. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the truth is the description, and the prophethood is the station, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of Abraham.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the truth, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own honesty. The believer is taught that the truth is the highest of the stations, and the one who lives the truth is the one whose truth the Lord values, and the believer who holds the honesty is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the description of Abraham is the model, the praise that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the opening is the believer whose honesty the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the station of the truth: Abraham was the man of the truth, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose station the Lord raises, and the opening is the teaching that the truth is the crown. The verse is the record of the opening, and the teaching of the truth that the station requires.",
  "42": "“˹Remember˺ when he said to his father, “O dear father! Why do you worship what can neither hear nor see, nor benefit you at all?”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the call: “Remember when he said to his father, ‘O dear father! Why do you worship what can neither hear nor see, nor benefit you at all?’” or “ith qala li-abeehi ya abati lima ta‘budu ma la yasma‘u wa la yubsiru wa la yughnee ‘anka shay’an,” and the commentators note the call, for Abraham addressed his father with the tenderness, and the question is the argument, the declaration that the idols cannot hear or see or benefit, and the verse is the record of the call, the argument that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the address \"ya abati\" — O dear father — is the tenderness, and the question is the argument of the reason, and the verse is the record of the call, the tenderness and the truth that Abraham joined. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the address is the tenderness, and the question is the argument, and the verse is the record of the call, the tenderness that the following verses display.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gentleness. The believer is taught that the call to the truth is the tenderness, and the one who calls the parent is the one whose call the Lord values, and the believer who argues with the gentleness is the believer whose argument the Lord accepts, and the call of Abraham is the model, the tenderness and the argument, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose gentleness the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the manner of the call: Abraham addressed the father with the tenderness, and the believer who calls the family is the believer whose tenderness the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the argument is the gentleness. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the gentleness that the calling requires.",
  "43": "“O dear father! I have certainly received some knowledge which you have not received, so follow me and I will guide you to the Straight Path.”” continues the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the call: “O dear father! I have certainly received some knowledge which you have not received, so follow me and I will guide you to the Straight Path,” or “ya abati innee qad ja’anee mina al-‘ilmi ma lam ya’tika fattabi‘nee ahdika siratan sawiyyan,” and the commentators note the call, for Abraham declared the knowledge and the guidance, and the call is the invitation, the offering of the path, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the call that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the knowledge is the revelation, and the guidance is the path, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the invitation of the father. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the knowledge is the gift, and the guidance is the path, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the call that the following verses display.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the guidance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the knowledge is the thing that the believer offers, and the one who guides to the path is the one whose guidance the Lord values, and the believer who calls with the knowledge is the believer whose call the Lord accepts, and the call of Abraham is the model, the offering of the knowledge and the guidance, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose calling the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the offering of the guidance: Abraham offered the knowledge and the path, and the believer who guides to the truth is the believer whose guidance the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the knowledge is the thing that the caller offers. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the guidance that the calling offers.",
  "44": "“O dear father! Do not worship Satan. Surely Satan is ever rebellious against the Most Compassionate.”” continues the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the call: “O dear father! Do not worship Satan. Surely Satan is ever rebellious against the Most Compassionate,” or “ya abati la ta‘budi ash-shaytana inna ash-shaytana kana lir-rahmani ‘asiyyan,” and the commentators note the call, for Abraham warned the father against the worship of Satan, and the warning is the teaching, the declaration that the worship of the idols is the worship of Satan, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the call that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the insight, the declaration that the idolatry is the obedience of the enemy, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the warning of the father. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the worship of Satan is the idolatry, and the rebellion is the state, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the warning that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own discernment. The believer is taught that the idolatry is the worship of the enemy, and the one who warns against it is the one whose discernment the Lord values, and the believer who knows the enemy is the believer whose knowledge the Lord accepts, and the warning of Abraham is the model, the insight of the prophet, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose discernment the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discernment of the enemy: Abraham warned against Satan, and the believer who knows the enemy is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the warning is the teaching that the idolatry is the worship of the enemy. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the discernment that the warning requires.",
  "45": "“O dear father! I truly fear that you will be touched by a torment from the Most Compassionate, and become Satan’s companion ˹in Hell˺.”” completes the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the call: “O dear father! I truly fear that you will be touched by a torment from the Most Compassionate, and become Satan’s companion in Hell,” or “ya abati innee akhafu an yamassaka ‘athabun mina ar-rahmani fa-takoona lish-shaytani waliyyan,” and the commentators note the completion, for Abraham warned the father of the torment, and the warning is the fear of the love, the declaration that the fear of the punishment is the concern of the son, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the following verse answers with the threat, and the commentators note the significance, for the fear of the son is the love, and the warning is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the love that the warning displayed. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the torment is the warning, and the companion of Satan is the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the father answered with the threat.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own concern. The believer is taught that the fear of the punishment for the other is the love, and the one who warns the loved is the one whose warning the Lord values, and the believer who cares for the family is the believer whose care the Lord accepts, and the warning of Abraham is the model, the fear of the love, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose concern the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the love of the warning: Abraham feared for the father, and the believer who warns the loved is the believer whose love the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the warning is the mercy. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the love that the warning displays.",
  "46": "“He threatened, “How dare you reject my idols, O Abraham! If you do not desist, I will certainly stone you ˹to death˺. So be gone from me for a long time!”” records the threat, and the commentators note the two parts of the threat. The verse records the threat: “How dare you reject my idols, O Abraham! If you do not desist, I will certainly stone you to death. So be gone from me for a long time!” or “qala a-raghibun anta ‘an alihatee ya Ibraheemu la-in lam tantahi la-arjumannaka wahjurnee maliyyan,” and the commentators note the threat, for the father threatened the stoning and the expulsion, and the threat is the answer of the falsehood, the violence that the argument could not supply, and the verse is the record of the threat, the answer that the following verse meets with the peace, and the commentators note the significance, for the threat of the father is the measure of the rejection, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence of the falsehood. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the stoning is the threat, and the expulsion is the separation, and the verse is the record of the threat, the answer that the following verse meets with the peace.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the threat, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the threat of the falsehood is the trial, and the one who bears the threat is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who answers the violence with the peace is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the threat of the father is the teaching, the declaration that the falsehood answers the argument with the violence, and the believer who reads the threat is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the threatened: Abraham was threatened and answered with the peace, and the believer who bears the threat is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the threat is the teaching that the falsehood is the violence. The verse is the record of the threat, and the teaching of the patience that the threat demands.",
  "47": "“Abraham responded, “Peace be upon you! I will pray to my Lord for your forgiveness. He has truly been Most Gracious to me.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “Peace be upon you! I will pray to my Lord for your forgiveness. He has truly been Most Gracious to me,” or “qala salamun ‘alayka sa-astaghfiru laka rabbee innahu kana bee hafiyyan,” and the commentators note the reply, for Abraham answered the threat with the peace, and the reply is the gentleness, the declaration that the believer answers the violence with the peace, and the prayer of the forgiveness is the promise, and the verse is the record of the reply, the gentleness that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the peace is the answer of the believer, and the prayer is the hope, and the verse is the record of the reply, the gentleness of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the peace is the answer, and the prayer is the hope, and the verse is the record of the reply, the gentleness that the following verse displays.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the peace, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gentleness. The believer is taught that the peace is the answer of the believer, and the one who answers the violence with the peace is the one whose gentleness the Lord values, and the believer who prays for the wrongdoer is the believer whose mercy the Lord accepts, and the reply of Abraham is the model, the peace of the prophet, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose gentleness the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the peace: Abraham answered the threat with the peace, and the believer who answers the violence with the gentleness is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the prayer is the teaching that the mercy is the answer of the believer. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the gentleness that the threat cannot break.",
  "48": "“As I distance myself from ˹all of˺ you and from whatever you invoke besides Allah, I will ˹continue to˺ call upon my Lord ˹alone˺, trusting that I will never be disappointed in invoking my Lord.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the reply: “As I distance myself from all of you and from whatever you invoke besides Allah, I will continue to call upon my Lord alone, trusting that I will never be disappointed in invoking my Lord,” or “wa a‘tazilukum wa ma tad‘oona min dooni Allahi wa ad‘oo rabbee ‘asa alla akoona bi-du‘a’i rabbee shaqiyyan,” and the commentators note the completion, for Abraham declared the separation and the reliance, and the verse is the record of the completion, the declaration that the believer separates from the falsehood and relies upon the Lord, and the commentators note the significance, for the separation is the departure, and the reliance is the trust, and the verse is the record of the completion, the declaration of the believer. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the separation is the departure, and the reliance is the trust, and the verse is the record of the completion, the declaration that the following verse fulfills with the grant.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the separation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the separation from the falsehood is the first step of the reliance, and the one who departs is the one whom the Lord receives, and the believer who calls upon the Lord alone is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the completion of Abraham is the model, the separation and the reliance, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose reliance the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the departure of the believer: Abraham separated and relied, and the believer who departs from the falsehood is the believer whom the Lord receives, and the completion is the teaching that the separation is the path of the reliance. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the reliance that the separation requires.",
  "49": "“So after he had left them and what they worshipped besides Allah, We granted him Isaac and Jacob, and made each of them a prophet.”” records the grant, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the grant: “So after he had left them and what they worshipped besides Allah, We granted him Isaac and Jacob, and made each of them a prophet,” or “fa-lamma i‘tazalahum wa ma ya‘budoona min dooni Allahi wahabna lahu Ishaqa wa Ya‘qooba wa kullan ja‘alna nabiyyan,” and the commentators note the grant, for the Lord granted Abraham the two sons and the prophethood, and the grant is the reward of the separation, and the verse is the record of the grant, the reward that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the grant of the sons is the compensation of the departure, and the verse is the record of the grant, the reward of the reliance. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the grant is the reward, and the reward is the compensation, and the verse is the record of the grant, the reward that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reward, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the separation from the falsehood is the thing that the Lord rewards, and the one who departs for the faith is the one whom the Lord compensates, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the grant of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the departure is the path of the reward, and the believer who reads the grant is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the departure: Abraham separated and was granted, and the believer who departs for the faith is the believer whom the Lord compensates, and the grant is the teaching that the reliance is the rewarded. The verse is the record of the grant, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "50": "“We showered them with Our mercy, and blessed them with honourable mention.”” completes the grant, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the grant: “We showered them with Our mercy, and blessed them with honourable mention,” or “wa wahabna lahum min rahmatina wa ja‘alna lahum lisana sidqin ‘aliyyan,” and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord showered the mercy and granted the honourable mention, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mercy and the mention that the Lord granted, and the commentators note the significance, for the \"lisana sidq\" — the tongue of the truth, the honourable mention — is the good name that the Lord granted, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mercy and the name that Abraham received. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mercy is the gift, and the mention is the name, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mercy and the name that the Lord granted.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mention, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the honourable mention is the gift, and the one whom the Lord mentions is the one whose name the Lord raises, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose mention the Lord grants, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the good name is the reward, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the truth: Abraham was granted the mention, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose name the Lord raises, and the completion is the teaching that the good name is the gift. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the mention carries.",
  "51": "“And mention in the Book ˹O Prophet, the story of˺ Moses. He was truly a chosen man, and was a messenger and a prophet.”” opens the account of Moses, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse commands the mention: “And mention in the Book O Prophet, the story of Moses. He was truly a chosen man, and was a messenger and a prophet,” or “wathkur fi al-kitabi Moosa innahu kana mukhlisan wa kana rasoolan nabiyyan,” and the commentators note the command, for the Prophet was commanded to mention Moses, and the description is the praise, the \"mukhlas,\" the chosen, the purified, and the \"rasool nabiyy,\" the messenger and the prophet, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the \"mukhlas\" is the chosen, the one whom the Lord purified for the mission, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the chosen is the purified, and the messenger is the sent, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the choosing, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sincerity. The believer is taught that the choosing of the Lord is the honour, and the one whom the Lord purifies is the one whose mission the Lord grants, and the believer who lives the sincerity is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the description of Moses is the model, the praise that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the opening is the believer whose sincerity the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honour of the choosing: Moses was the chosen, and the believer who lives the sincerity is the believer whom the Lord chooses, and the opening is the teaching that the sincerity is the path of the mission. The verse is the record of the opening, and the teaching of the sincerity that the choosing requires.",
  "52": "“We called him from the right side of Mount Ṭûr, and drew him near, speaking ˹with him˺ directly.”” records the calling, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the calling: “We called him from the right side of Mount Tur, and drew him near, speaking with him directly,” or “wa nadaynahu min janibi at-toori al-aymani wa qarrabnahu najiyyan,” and the commentators note the calling, for the Lord called Moses from the side of the mount, and the drawing near is the honour, the direct speech, and the verse is the record of the calling, the honour that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the calling from the right side is the honour, and the direct speech is the station, and the verse is the record of the calling, the honour that Moses received. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the calling is the honour, and the drawing near is the station, and the verse is the record of the calling, the honour that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the nearness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the nearness of the Lord is the honour, and the one whom the Lord draws near is the one whose station the Lord raises, and the believer who seeks the nearness is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the calling of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the Lord draws near the chosen, and the believer who reads the calling is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nearness of the Lord: Moses was drawn near, and the believer who seeks the nearness is the believer whom the Lord draws, and the calling is the teaching that the direct speech is the honour of the chosen. The verse is the record of the calling, and the teaching of the nearness that the chosen receive.",
  "53": "“And We appointed for him—out of Our grace—his brother, Aaron, as a prophet.”” records the appointment, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the appointment: “And We appointed for him—out of Our grace—his brother, Aaron, as a prophet,” or “wa wahabna lahu min rahmatina akhahu Haroona nabiyyan,” and the commentators note the appointment, for the Lord granted Aaron as the prophet, and the grant is the mercy, the answer of the prayer of Moses, and the verse is the record of the appointment, the mercy that the Lord granted, and the commentators note the significance, for the appointment of Aaron is the answer of the prayer, the grant of the helper, and the verse is the record of the appointment, the mercy that the following verse parallels. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the appointment is the mercy, and the grant is the answer, and the verse is the record of the appointment, the mercy that the Lord granted to Moses.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the grace, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the grace of the Lord is the grant, and the one who prays for the helper is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who seeks the grace is the believer whose grace the Lord grants, and the appointment of Aaron is the teaching, the declaration that the grace of the Lord is the answer of the prayer, and the believer who reads the appointment is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the grace of the Lord: Aaron was granted, and the believer who prays for the help is the believer whose prayer the Lord answers, and the appointment is the teaching that the grace is the grant. The verse is the record of the appointment, and the teaching of the hope that the grace carries.",
  "54": "“And mention in the Book ˹O Prophet, the story of˺ Ishmael. He was truly a man of his word, and was a messenger and a prophet.”” opens the account of Ishmael, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse commands the mention: “And mention in the Book O Prophet, the story of Ishmael. He was truly a man of his word, and was a messenger and a prophet,” or “wathkur fi al-kitabi Isma‘eela innahu kana sadiqa al-wa‘di wa kana rasoolan nabiyyan,” and the commentators note the command, for the Prophet was commanded to mention Ishmael, and the description is the praise, the \"sadiq al-wa‘d,\" the man of the word, the one who keeps the promise, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of Ishmael, and the commentators note the significance, for the \"sadiq al-wa‘d\" is the fidelity, the description that the following verse completes, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fidelity is the description, and the prophethood is the station, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of Ishmael.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fidelity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own promise. The believer is taught that the keeping of the word is the mark of the believer, and the one who keeps the promise is the one whose fidelity the Lord values, and the believer who honours the word is the believer whose promise the Lord accepts, and the description of Ishmael is the model, the praise that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the opening is the believer whose fidelity the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the fidelity of the word: Ishmael was the man of the word, and the believer who keeps the promise is the believer whose fidelity the Lord values, and the opening is the teaching that the word is the trust. The verse is the record of the opening, and the teaching of the fidelity that the word requires.",
  "55": "“He used to urge his people to pray and give alms-tax. And his Lord was well pleased with him.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: “He used to urge his people to pray and give alms-tax. And his Lord was well pleased with him,” or “wa kana ya’muru ahlahu bis-salati waz-zakati wa kana ‘inda rabbihi mardiyyan,” and the commentators note the completion, for Ishmael urged the prayer and the zakah, and the pleasure of the Lord is the reward, and the verse is the record of the completion, the description and the reward that the story closes with, and the commentators note the significance, for the urging of the family is the duty, and the pleasure of the Lord is the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reward of the fidelity. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the urging is the duty, and the pleasure is the reward, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reward that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the urging, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the urging of the family to the prayer is the duty, and the one who commands the good is the one whose command the Lord values, and the believer who seeks the pleasure of the Lord is the believer whose seeking the Lord accepts, and the completion of Ishmael is the model, the urging and the pleasure, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose duty the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the family: Ishmael urged the prayer, and the believer who commands the good is the believer whose duty the Lord values, and the pleasure of the Lord is the teaching that the end is the acceptance. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the duty that the family requires.",
  "56": "“And mention in the Book ˹O Prophet, the story of˺ Enoch. He was surely a man of truth and a prophet.”” opens the account of Enoch, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse commands the mention: “And mention in the Book O Prophet, the story of Enoch. He was surely a man of truth and a prophet,” or “wathkur fi al-kitabi Idreesa innahu kana siddeeqan nabiyyan,” and the commentators note the command, for the Prophet was commanded to mention Enoch, and the description is the praise, the \"siddeeq nabiyy,\" the man of the truth and the prophet, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of Enoch, and the commentators note the significance, for Enoch is the Idris, the one of the elevated station, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction that the following verse completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the truth is the description, and the prophethood is the station, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of Enoch.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the truth, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own honesty. The believer is taught that the truth is the station, and the one who lives the truth is the one whose truth the Lord values, and the believer who holds the honesty is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the description of Enoch is the model, the praise that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the opening is the believer whose honesty the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the station of the truth: Enoch was the man of the truth, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose station the Lord raises, and the opening is the teaching that the truth is the crown. The verse is the record of the opening, and the teaching of the truth that the station requires.",
  "57": "“And We elevated him to an honourable status.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the account: “And We elevated him to an honourable status,” or “wa rafa‘nahu makanan ‘aliyyan,” and the commentators note the elevation, for the Lord raised Enoch to the honourable status, and the elevation is the reward, the grant of the high station, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reward that the story closes with, and the commentators note the significance, for the elevation of Enoch is the station of the heavens, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reward of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the elevation is the reward, and the status is the high, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reward that the Lord granted.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the elevation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the elevation of the Lord is the reward, and the one whom the Lord raises is the one whose station the Lord grants, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whose elevation the Lord grants, and the completion of Enoch is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the path of the elevation, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the truth: Enoch was elevated, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whom the Lord raises, and the completion is the teaching that the elevation is the reward of the honesty. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the elevation carries.",
  "58": "\"Those were some of the prophets who Allah has blessed from among the descendants of Adam, and of those We carried with Noah in the Ark, and of the descendants of Abraham and Israel, and of those We rightly guided and chose. Whenever the revelations of the Most Compassionate were recited to them, they fell down, prostrating and weeping\" gathers the prophets together and describes their common response to God's word.\n\nThe verse names the lineages from which the prophets came: Adam, the passengers of Noah's ark, Abraham, and Israel, which is Jacob. These are the major channels through which prophethood flowed. The prophets are not scattered random figures. They form a connected chain of blessing stretching from the beginning of humanity.\n\nAll of them shared one quality: when the revelations of God were recited to them, they fell down prostrating and weeping. Prostration is the highest physical expression of submission. Weeping is the highest emotional expression of awe and love. Together, they describe the prophets' complete response to God's word.\n\nThe prophets were not cold intellectuals who analyzed revelation without feeling. They trembled at it. They wept. They put their faces on the ground. Their minds understood the word, and their hearts were moved by it.\n\nThis verse is also connected to the prostrations of recitation in Islamic practice. When a Muslim recites or hears this verse, they perform a prostration, following the example of the prophets.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a challenge. When you hear the Quran, how do you respond? Do you listen with distraction, with intellectual analysis only, or with a heart that is moved? The prophets fell down weeping. Ask God to give you a heart that responds to His word as they did.\n\nThe list also closes with the genealogy of the chapter itself. Adam, Noah's ark, Abraham, Israel: the same names the surah has been visiting since Zachariah prayed. The gallery was not a random selection but a family tree, and the weeping listeners in it are the ancestors and kin of the Prophet's own audience, which makes the challenge at the end personal rather than abstract.",
  "59": "\"But they were succeeded by generations who neglected prayer and followed their lusts and so will soon face the evil consequences\" describes what happened after the time of the prophets. The previous verse honored the prophets. This verse describes those who came after them and fell away.\n\nThe word \"succeeded\" means that later generations took the place of the prophets and their righteous followers. They inherited the scripture and the community, but they did not inherit the faith that went with them. Outward continuity is not enough. Inner devotion is what matters.\n\nThey neglected prayer. The Arabic word does not mean they completely abandoned it, though some did. It means they were careless with it. They prayed late, prayed without attention, or prayed only to be seen. The first sign of a decaying community is neglect of the prayer, because prayer is the backbone of the faith.\n\nThey also followed their lusts. They chased their desires, whether for wealth, status, food, or sexual pleasure. Their lives were governed by what they wanted rather than by what God commanded. This is the opposite of the prophetic way, which is governed by God's will.\n\nThe result is that they will \"face the evil consequences.\" The Arabic word, \"ghayy,\" is sometimes interpreted as the name of a valley in Hell, or as a general term for evil and perdition. Either way, the meaning is clear: those who neglect prayer and follow desire are heading for ruin.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a serious warning. Do not assume that you are safe because you inherited the religion or live in a Muslim community. The faith of your ancestors does not save you if you neglect your own prayers and follow your own desires. Hold fast to the prayer, and resist the pull of lust.",
  "60": "\"As for those who repent, believe, and do good, it is they who will be admitted into Paradise, never being denied any reward\" gives hope after the warning. The previous verse described those who neglected prayer and followed lust. This verse describes those who turn back.\n\nThe path back has three steps. The first is repentance, or \"tawbah.\" This means feeling regret for the sin, stopping it immediately, and resolving not to return to it. Repentance is the door through which a person returns to God.\n\nThe second is belief. Repentance without faith is incomplete. A person must believe in God, in the Hereafter, and in the message of the Prophet. Faith is the foundation upon which a changed life is built.\n\nThe third is doing good. True repentance shows itself in action. A person who says they have repented but continues in the same behavior has not truly repented. Good deeds are the fruit of repentance and faith.\n\nThe reward is Paradise, and the promise is that they will never be denied any reward. Every good they did will be accepted. Every step back to God will be honored. Even the sins they left behind will be replaced by good deeds, as the Quran elsewhere says.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a wide-open door. If you have been neglectful, if you have followed your desires, if you feel far from God, know that the door of repentance is open. Turn back, believe, and do good. Paradise is waiting, and God's reward is not withheld from anyone who sincerely returns.\n\n'Never being denied any reward' also answers a fear the previous verse creates. A person who wasted years in neglect may assume the past permanently disqualifies him, and the verse removes that assumption in four words. The debt of heedlessness is real, but the door described here is not a reduced entrance; it is Paradise, whole, with nothing of the reward withheld from the one who returned.",
  "61": "\"They will be in the Gardens of Eternity, promised in trust by the Most Compassionate to His servants. Surely His promise will be fulfilled\" describes the home of the repentant believers. The previous verse said they would enter Paradise. This verse gives the garden a name and explains why it is certain.\n\nThe gardens are called \"Gardens of Eternity,\" or \"Jannat Adn.\" The word \"Adn\" means perpetual abode. These gardens are not temporary resting places. They are the permanent home of the righteous, where there is no death, no aging, and no departure.\n\nThey were \"promised in trust by the Most Compassionate to His servants.\" The phrase \"in trust\" means that the promise was made in the unseen, before the believers ever saw it. They did not earn Paradise through their deeds alone. They entered it because God, in His mercy, promised it to them. Their deeds were a means, but the promise was a gift.\n\nThe name \"Most Compassionate\" is repeated throughout this chapter, tying the stories of Zachariah, Mary, Abraham, and the other prophets together. All of them received from the mercy of the Most Compassionate. The gardens of Paradise are the final expression of that mercy.\n\nThe verse ends with \"Surely His promise will be fulfilled.\" God never breaks His word. What He has promised will come to pass, even if it feels distant. The believer trusts the promise more than they trust what their eyes see in this world.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a source of hope. If you have repented, believed, and done good, then a garden has been promised to you. You have not seen it yet, but it is more real than the room you are sitting in. Walk toward it with patience, knowing that the One who made the promise is faithful to keep it.",
  "62": "\"There they will never hear any idle talk—only greetings of peace. And there they will have their provisions morning and evening\" describes the life of Paradise. The previous verse said the believers will enter the Gardens of Eternity. This verse gives a taste of what life there is like.\n\nFirst, there is no idle talk. The Arabic word, \"laghw,\" means any speech that is empty, pointless, false, or hurtful. In Paradise, every word has meaning, and every conversation is good. There are no lies, no gossip, no arguments, no insults. The heart is at rest because the tongue is pure.\n\nInstead of idle talk, they hear \"greetings of peace.\" The angels greet them with peace. They greet one another with peace. God Himself greets them with a word of peace. The word \"salam\" means wholeness, safety, and well-being. It is the perfect greeting for a place where every wound is healed.\n\nThey receive their provision \"morning and evening.\" This does not mean that Paradise has a sun or a cycle of day and night as we know it. It means that provision comes at known times, continuously, without end. The believers eat and drink whenever they wish, in a state of perpetual ease.\n\nThe mention of morning and evening also connects this life with the next. In this world, the believer eats morning and evening and remembers God. In the next, that pattern continues, but without labor, without scarcity, and without the fear that the provision will end.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to imagine a place where speech is pure, where peace is constant, and where provision never fails. That place is real. Let your heart long for it, and let that longing shape how you speak, how you treat others, and how you remember God in your mornings and evenings.",
  "63": "\"That is Paradise, which We will grant to whoever is devout among Our servants\" states who receives the home described in the previous verses. The beautiful descriptions of Paradise are not for everyone. They are for a specific kind of person.\n\nThe key word is \"devout,\" which translates \"muttaqin,\" those who have taqwa. Taqwa means being mindful of God, avoiding what He has forbidden, and guarding oneself from His punishment. The people of Paradise are not merely those who say they believe. They are those who live with consciousness of God.\n\nTaqwa includes the fear of God, but it is more than fear. It is a carefulness about how you live. A person with taqwa thinks before they speak, checks their intentions before they act, and turns back to God when they slip. They treat others with fairness because they know God is watching.\n\nThe verse says Paradise is granted, not earned. This is the balance of Islamic teaching. No one enters Paradise through their deeds alone. They enter through God's mercy. But God's mercy is given to those who prepare for it through devotion. The devout are the ones who open the door.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to examine yourself. Are you among the devout? Do you live mindful of God? Do you avoid what He has forbidden? Do you return to Him when you fall? If not, the door is still open. Turn to Him now, and seek the taqwa that leads to Paradise.\n\n'Among Our servants' adds the relationship the word 'devout' alone would miss. These people are not strangers who impressed heaven with their performance; they are servants whose carefulness grew out of service. The verse describes a household, and the inheritance of Paradise is placed within it, which keeps the promise intimate rather than contractual. The same possessive runs through this chapter's portraits of the prophets, and the tone is that of a Lord speaking about His own.",
  "64": "\"We only descend by the command of your Lord. To Him belongs whatever is before us, and whatever is behind us, and everything in between. And your Lord is never forgetful\" records the words of the angel Gabriel to the Prophet. The previous verses described Paradise. This verse explains something about how revelation comes.\n\nThe Prophet is reported to have waited for a period without revelation, and when Gabriel came, he asked why he did not visit more often. This verse was the answer. The angels do not descend on their own initiative. They come only by the command of God. The timing of revelation is in God's hands.\n\nThe phrase \"whatever is before us, and whatever is behind us, and everything in between\" means that God knows all of time: the future, the past, and the present. Nothing is hidden from Him. The angels act within His knowledge and under His command.\n\nThe final words, \"your Lord is never forgetful,\" are a comfort. The gap between revelations was not because God forgot the Prophet. God does not forget. Every delay has a wisdom. The Prophet was being taught to wait, and the wisdom of the waiting would become clear in time.\n\nThis verse also teaches something about the nature of prophecy. The Prophet did not control the revelation. He did not decide when Gabriel would come or what would be revealed. He was entirely dependent on God. This is the mark of a true prophet: he delivers what is given to him, nothing more and nothing less.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder that God's timing is perfect. There may be periods in your life when you feel that guidance or help is delayed. In those moments, remember that your Lord is never forgetful. He knows what is before you, what is behind you, and what lies between. He sends help exactly when it is needed.",
  "65": "\"He is the Lord of the heavens, and the earth, and everything in between. So worship Him alone, and be steadfast in His worship. Do you know of anyone equal to Him in His attributes?\" follows the angel's words with a direct call to the Prophet and to all believers.\n\nThe first statement establishes the foundation of worship: God is the Lord of everything. The heavens, the earth, and all that lies between them belong to Him. He created them, sustains them, and governs them. If He is the Lord of all, then He alone deserves worship.\n\nThe command is to worship Him \"alone.\" This is the same call given by every prophet. Worship is not merely ritual. It is devoting your entire life to God, loving Him, fearing Him, hoping in Him, and obeying Him without partners.\n\nThe second command is to \"be steadfast in His worship.\" The word for steadfastness, \"istabir,\" implies persistence, patience, and endurance. Worship is not a single act. It is a lifelong practice. You pray even when you do not feel like it. You give even when money is tight. You fast even when it is hard. Steadfastness is what separates occasional piety from a life of faith.\n\nThe closing question is powerful: \"Do you know of anyone equal to Him in His attributes?\" The answer is no. No one is like God. No one shares His knowledge, His power, or His mercy. He is unique, and the worship of any being besides Him is a failure to recognize who He truly is.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a summary of the entire Islamic message. Know that God is the Lord of all. Worship Him alone. Be steadfast. And never imagine that anything in creation could be equal to Him.\n\nThe closing question also works as a test any listener can run. It does not ask for expert theology; it asks whether anyone comes to mind who shares His attributes, and the mind searches and returns empty. That emptiness is the argument, and it is available to a shepherd in the desert and a scholar in the city alike.",
  "66": "\"Yet some people ask mockingly, 'After I die, will I really be raised to life again?'\" records the skepticism of the disbelievers. The previous verses called to worship and steadfastness. This verse shows how some people respond.\n\nThe question about resurrection is asked \"mockingly.\" The disbelievers do not genuinely wonder. They use the question to ridicule the Prophet and the believers. They find the idea of life after death absurd, and they express their contempt through laughter and challenge.\n\nThis same question has been asked in every age. People look at the decay of the body and find it hard to believe that the same person can be restored. The Quran answers this objection by pointing to the first creation. If God made you from nothing, He can certainly bring you back after you have turned to dust.\n\nThe mockery in the question is significant. A sincere question deserves an answer. A mocking question deserves a warning. The disbelievers are not seeking truth. They are seeking to dismiss it.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a challenge to examine your own heart. Do you believe in the Hereafter as a certainty, or is it a distant idea you rarely think about? The question of resurrection is not academic. How you live depends on whether you believe you will be raised. If you truly believe that every action will be accounted for, your life will look very different from the life of someone who thinks death is the end.\n\n'After I die' also reveals the assumption beneath the mockery. The questioner treats death as the finish line, the point past which nothing is owed, and the Quran spends entire passages unseating that picture. A person is not owed a second life because he deserves one; he is raised because the One who created him has promised it, and the promise-keeper is not the mocker.",
  "67": "\"Do such people not remember that We created them before, when they were nothing?\" answers the mockery with a simple argument. The previous verse recorded the question about resurrection. This verse points back to the first creation.\n\nThe word \"remember\" means to recall, reflect, and take heed. The disbelievers are being asked to think about their own origin. They were not always here. There was a time when they did not exist. Then God brought them into being.\n\nThe phrase \"when they were nothing\" is humbling. Every human being began as nothing. No person created themselves. No person chose their parents, their time of birth, or their basic nature. We are all creatures, entirely dependent on the One who made us.\n\nIf God could create us from nothing, then raising us after death is not difficult. The first creation is actually the greater miracle. A second creation is simply a return to something that already existed. The God who made a person from dust can reform that person from dust.\n\nThis is one of the Quran's most frequent arguments for the resurrection. It does not require complicated philosophy. It simply asks: look at yourself. You exist. God made you. Why would you think He cannot remake you?\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a meditation on your own existence. The fact that you are here, reading these words, is a sign of God's power. The same power that gave you life will give you life again. Let that truth settle your doubts and strengthen your faith.\n\nThe argument also lands personally rather than abstractly. It does not cite the creation of the heavens or the revival of the earth after rain, though the Quran uses both elsewhere; it cites the questioner's own beginning. Of all the evidences available, the one a person cannot distance himself from is himself, and the verse puts that evidence directly into the question.",
  "68": "\"By your Lord O Prophet! We will surely gather them along with the devils, and then set them around Hell on their knees\" swears by the Lord of the Prophet that the disbelievers will be gathered. The previous verse argued from creation. This verse gives a solemn oath about the Hereafter.\n\nThe oath \"By your Lord\" is one of the strongest forms of assertion in Arabic. God is swearing by His own lordship, and the matter is so certain that it is stated without hesitation. The disbelievers will be resurrected.\n\nThey will be gathered \"along with the devils.\" This means that those who followed the devils in this world will be with them in the next. The leaders and the followers, the tempters and the tempted, will all be assembled together. The false alliances of this world will become the shared fate of the Hereafter.\n\nThey will be set around Hell \"on their knees.\" The image is one of complete humiliation. In this world, the disbelievers walked arrogantly, sat on thrones, and looked down on others. In the next, they are on their knees, surrounded by the Fire they denied.\n\nThe posture of kneeling also reflects their powerlessness. They cannot run, cannot argue, and cannot stand. Every pretense of strength is gone.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a stark reminder of the reality of judgment. The arrogance of this life will not last. The people who seem most powerful may be the most humiliated on the Day when every knee bends before God. Do not be impressed by the swagger of the world. Remember what awaits.\n\n'Along with the devils' also completes an account the Quran keeps. In this life the two parties are separated by nature: one whispers from the unseen, the other acts in the open, and each can blame the other. The gathering described here ends that arrangement, and the record elsewhere in the Quran has Satan denying that he ever had authority over anyone, so the partners meet with the excuse already demolished.",
  "69": "\"Then We will certainly begin by dragging out of every group the ones most defiant to the Most Compassionate\" describes the order of punishment. The previous verse said the disbelievers will be gathered around Hell. This verse says who is taken first.\n\nOut of every group, community, or nation, the \"most defiant\" will be dragged out first. These are the leaders in disbelief, the ones who called others to evil, the arrogant oppressors, and the scholars who corrupted the truth. They bear a greater responsibility because they misled others.\n\nThe word \"dragging\" is humbling. They are not politely escorted. They are pulled, as criminals are pulled to punishment. The same people who were carried on thrones in this world will be dragged on their faces in the next.\n\nTheir defiance was specifically \"to the Most Compassionate.\" They rebelled against the God of mercy, rejected His messengers, and mocked His revelations. Their defiance was not against an impersonal force. It was against the One who showed them mercy every day of their lives.\n\nThis verse also establishes a principle of justice: punishment corresponds to the level of sin and the degree of defiance. The leaders in evil receive the worst punishment. The followers may be punished less, but they are not excused for following evil.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning to anyone in a position of influence. If you lead others away from God, your punishment will be greater than theirs. Use whatever platform you have to call people to truth, not to defiance.\n\n'The ones most defiant' also implies a scale rather than a single crime. Defiance has degrees, and the verse measures punishment against them, which is why leaders are named first. A reckoning that sentenced the recruiter and the recruit identically would be treating unequal things equally; the ordering here protects precisely the distinction that fair justice requires.",
  "70": "\"And We truly know best who is most deserving of burning in it\" follows the description of dragging out the defiant. This verse affirms God's complete knowledge of who deserves punishment.\n\nThe phrase \"most deserving of burning\" means that God knows exactly who should enter Hell and in what order. There is no random selection, no mistake, and no injustice. Every person receives exactly what their deeds warrant.\n\nGod's knowledge is perfect. He knows the hidden motives, the secret deeds, the influence a person had on others, and the exact weight of every sin. No one will be punished more than they deserve. No one will be punished less. The justice of the Hereafter is absolute.\n\nThis verse is a comfort for the oppressed and a warning for the oppressor. In this life, some crimes go unpunished and some innocent people suffer. But God knows who is most deserving of what, and His judgment is final. Nothing is hidden from Him.\n\nThe word \"burning\" refers to Hell itself. The Quran uses vivid language about the Fire so that people will take it seriously. It is not a metaphor for a temporary state of mind. It is a real place of real punishment.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to trust God's justice. If you have been wronged, know that the wrongdoer will not escape. If you have wronged others, know that repentance is still possible before the Day when God's perfect knowledge assigns every person their place.\n\n'Knows best' also settles a competition the earlier verse started. Dragging out the most defiant group by group could suggest a visible ranking that observers might dispute, with each person arguing that another was worse. The verse removes the debate in advance: the complete record already exists, and the One holding it does not need the tribunal's help to read it.",
  "71": "\"There is none of you who will not pass over it. This is a decree your Lord must fulfil\" states that every human being will pass over Hell. The previous verses described the gathering and the dragging of the defiant. This verse says that all people, righteous and wicked, will approach the Fire.\n\nThe \"passing over\" is traditionally understood as crossing the bridge, or \"sirat,\" that spans over Hell. On the Day of Judgment, all humanity will cross this bridge. The righteous will pass over it swiftly, according to their deeds, and enter Paradise. The wicked will fall from it into Hell.\n\nThe verse is clear that no one is exempt. \"There is none of you who will not pass over it.\" This includes the prophets, the righteous, and the sinners. Everyone approaches the Fire, but not everyone enters it. The passing over is part of the terror of the Day, but for the believers it becomes the moment of deliverance.\n\nThe phrase \"a decree your Lord must fulfil\" means that this is fixed and certain. It is not a possibility. It is a decree. God has spoken, and what He has decreed will happen.\n\nThis teaching should not cause despair for the believer. Rather, it should increase consciousness of God and reliance on His mercy. The believer knows that they will approach the Fire, but they also know that their faith and good deeds are the means by which God carries them across.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to prepare. Every soul will face that crossing. What matters is which side you reach on the other side. Live a life that will carry you safely over.\n\n'None of you' is addressed, scholars note, to all beings gathered that Day, and the universality is the point. The Fire is not a spectacle some watch from a safe distance; every soul passes near enough to know exactly what it is, and deliverance is felt as deliverance only by those who stood at the same edge as everyone else.",
  "72": "\"Then We will deliver those who were devout, leaving the wrongdoers there on their knees\" describes what happens after the crossing. The previous verse said everyone passes over. This verse says who is saved.\n\nGod \"delivers\" the devout, those who had taqwa. Their deliverance is an act of His mercy. They do not save themselves. Their faith and deeds are the reason they are delivered, but salvation itself is from God.\n\nThe wrongdoers are left in Hell, on their knees. The same posture described in verse 68 returns. They have no strength to stand, no one to help them, and no way out. They remain exactly where their deeds have led them.\n\nThe contrast between the two groups is sharp. The devout are delivered. The wrongdoers are left. This is the final separation. In this life, the righteous and the wicked live side by side. In the Hereafter, they are divided forever.\n\nThe word \"deliver\" also implies that the believers pass through a danger. Salvation is not merely the absence of punishment. It is rescue from a real threat. The believer should feel a sense of awe that they were delivered from what could have been their fate.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is both a hope and a warning. If you are among the devout, God will deliver you. If you remain among the wrongdoers, you will be left where you fall. Choose taqwa now, while there is still time to cross to safety.\n\nThe order of events also carries its own message. Deliverance comes after the crossing, not instead of it, and the devout are rescued from a place they actually stood, not spared the sight of it. What the verse removes from the believer is not the exposure but the outcome. In the Quran's picture of that Day, the ones led out of danger weep with relief, and the weeping is part of the scene.",
  "73": "\"When Our clear revelations are recited to them, the disbelievers ask the believers mockingly, 'Which of the two of us is better in status and superior in assembly?'\" records another arrogant question from the disbelievers. The previous verses described the Hereafter. This verse returns to the attitudes of this world.\n\nWhen the Quran is recited to them, the disbelievers do not reflect. They compare themselves to the believers on the basis of worldly status. They point to their wealth, their homes, their gatherings, and their influence, and ask who is really better off.\n\nThe question is mocking. They assume that worldly prosperity is evidence of being favored by God. Because they have more money and larger gatherings, they conclude that they must be on the right path and the believers must be wrong.\n\nThis is the same argument made by the wealthy man in the parable of the two gardens in Surah al-Kahf. He looked at his wealth and status and felt superior to his believing companion. The Quran repeatedly warns against measuring truth by worldly success.\n\nThe phrase \"superior in assembly\" refers to the quality of the people who gather around them. The disbelievers have nobles, chiefs, and wealthy companions. The believers are often poor, former slaves, and young people. The disbelievers use this difference to claim superiority.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning against judging by appearances. The size of a gathering, the elegance of a building, and the wealth of the people in it tell you nothing about whether they are guided. Truth is not determined by status. The next verse answers their arrogance directly.\n\n'When Our clear revelations are recited' also fixes the timing of the comparison. The question comes at the moment of hearing truth, which suggests the boasting functions as a defence. A person secure in his position does not need to announce it every time a verse is read; the reflex to compare wealth at the point of revelation betrays the discomfort underneath.",
  "74": "\"Imagine, O Prophet, how many peoples We have destroyed before them, who were far better in luxury and splendour!\" answers the disbelievers' comparison by pointing to history. The previous verse recorded their pride in their status. This verse reminds them of what happened to peoples who had far more.\n\nThe command to \"imagine\" invites reflection on the ruins of earlier nations. Many peoples before the Quraysh were wealthier, more powerful, and more splendid. They had greater palaces, larger armies, and finer possessions. Yet they were destroyed when they rejected their messengers.\n\nThe word \"luxury\" refers to the comforts of life. The word \"splendour\" refers to outward appearance. The earlier nations had both. They had everything the Quraysh were boasting about, and more. But none of it saved them from God's punishment.\n\nThe verse does not merely mention these nations in passing. It says \"imagine\" them. The Quran wants the listener to picture the ruins, to reflect on the empty palaces and the abandoned homes. That visual reflection is more powerful than any abstract argument.\n\nThe lesson is clear. Worldly prosperity is not evidence of divine pleasure. If it were, the destroyed nations would not have been destroyed. Wealth and status are tests, not proofs. They can be taken as quickly as they are given.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to keep the right perspective. Do not be impressed by luxury. Do not measure people by their splendour. Look at the fate of those who had more of both and refused to believe. Their wealth did not save them, and neither will yours.\n\n'Far better' is also stated as a plain comparative, not a flourish. The Quran is not consoling the poor with a vague promise that riches do not matter; it concedes the scale and then wins it: the destroyed peoples had measurably more, and it did not purchase them a single hour. The argument works precisely because the comparison is granted rather than denied.",
  "75": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Whoever is entrenched in misguidance, the Most Compassionate will allow them plenty of time, until—behold!—they face what they are threatened with: either the torment or the Hour. Only then will they realize who is worse in position and inferior in manpower'\" gives the Prophet a response to the disbelievers' arrogance. The previous verse pointed to destroyed nations. This verse explains why God allows the misguided to prosper.\n\nThe phrase \"entrenched in misguidance\" describes someone deeply settled in error. They are not on the edge. They are fully committed to the wrong path. For such people, God allows them \"plenty of time.\" He extends their rope, gives them respite, and lets them continue.\n\nThis respite is not a blessing. It is a gradual leading on. The more time they have, the deeper they sink and the greater their punishment becomes. The Quran warns in another verse that when God gives the wrongdoer time, it is only so that they increase in sin.\n\nEventually, they face what they were threatened with: either torment in this life, like the destroyed nations, or the Hour of Judgment. At that point, they finally know \"who is worse in position and inferior in manpower.\" The tables are turned. The wealth and the gatherings they boasted about are gone, and they stand alone before God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning not to mistake God's patience for approval. If you are on the wrong path and things seem to be going well, do not assume you are safe. The rope may be long, but it has an end. Turn back before the day when your position and your manpower will be useless.\n\n'Say' also makes the answer a public one. God does not tell the Prophet to think this silently or to wait for history to prove the point; the words are put in his mouth for delivery to the boasters themselves, which is itself a mercy. Even the man being led on is told, plainly, that he is being led on.",
  "76": "\"And Allah increases in guidance those who are rightly guided. And the everlasting good deeds are far better with your Lord in reward and in outcome\" balances the previous verse with good news for the guided. The previous verse said the misguided are given time. This verse says the guided are given more guidance.\n\nGuidance is not static. It grows. A person who responds to the guidance they have been given receives more. A person who acts on what they know is shown more truth. This is a spiritual law: use it or lose it. The one who walks toward God finds God drawing near.\n\nThe phrase \"everlasting good deeds\" refers to the deeds whose reward continues, such as ongoing charity, beneficial knowledge, and righteous children who pray for their parents. It also refers to every good deed done sincerely for God, which remains in the Hereafter when worldly possessions vanish.\n\nThese good deeds are \"far better with your Lord in reward and in outcome.\" Their reward is greater than any worldly gain, and their outcome is better. Wealth disappears, status fades, but good deeds remain and multiply.\n\nThe contrast with the previous verse is important. The misguided receive more time to increase in misguidance. The guided receive more guidance to increase in good. Both processes are real. Which one you experience depends on the direction of your heart.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an encouragement. If you are on the path of guidance, keep walking. God will increase you. Do not look at the temporary prosperity of the misguided with envy. The lasting reward of good deeds is far better.\n\nThe word 'everlasting' also does quiet work against the boasting recorded just before. Wealth and sons were the two things the disbeliever trusted, and both are famously temporary; the verse answers with a category that does not decay. It is a direct comparison run between two portfolios, one of which is gone within a generation.",
  "77": "\"Have you seen O Prophet the one who rejects Our revelations yet boasts, 'I will definitely be granted plenty of wealth and children if there is an afterlife'?'\" introduces a specific disbeliever who combined rejection of God's signs with arrogant claims about the Hereafter. Early commentators identified him as a wealthy Meccan leader who boasted that even if there were a resurrection, he would be wealthy there too.\n\nThis man's arrogance is extraordinary. He rejects the clear revelations of God. Yet he assumes that if there is another life, he will be favored in it. He treats the Hereafter as if it operates by the same rules as this world, where wealth and children can be passed down or earned.\n\nThe word \"boasts\" shows that this is not a humble hope. It is a proud claim. He is so convinced of his own importance that he imagines God will honor him in the next life despite his disbelief.\n\nThis attitude is not unique to one Meccan. People in every age assume that their comfort in this world is a sign that they will be comfortable in the next. They live however they wish and then say, \"God will forgive me,\" without any evidence or repentance.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning against presumption. Do not assume that you will be granted wealth, children, or Paradise in the Hereafter while rejecting God's revelations. The next life is not an extension of this one. Its currency is faith and deeds, not bank accounts and family names.\n\n'Have you seen' also frames the boast as a spectacle. The Quran regularly uses this expression to turn a person's claim into something the listener inspects from the outside, and inspected claims look different from spoken ones. The man is presented not as an argument to answer but as a sight to consider, and consideration is all the refutation he needs.",
  "78": "\"Has he looked into the unseen or taken a pledge from the Most Compassionate?\" challenges the disbeliever's claim with two sharp questions. The previous verse recorded his boast. This verse exposes its emptiness.\n\nThe first question asks if he has looked into the unseen. In other words, does he have knowledge of the future? Has God told him that he will be granted wealth and children? Of course not. He is making a claim based on nothing.\n\nThe second question asks if he has taken a pledge from the Most Compassionate. A pledge is a covenant or a guarantee. Does he have a promise from God that he will be saved and blessed? The answer is again no. No such pledge exists.\n\nA person who claims something about the unseen without revelation is guessing. A person who claims God's favor without obedience is making an empty boast. This man had neither knowledge nor a covenant. He only had his own arrogance.\n\nThe verse is a warning against claiming to know what God will do in the Hereafter based on personal assumptions. The only thing we know about the Hereafter is what God and His Prophet have told us. Any claim beyond that is speculation.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to humility before God. Do not claim to know what you do not know. Do not assume a guarantee that you have not been given. Put your hope in God's mercy, but do so through faith and obedience, not through arrogant claims.\n\nThe two questions also cover the only two ways anyone could know the unseen. Either he has seen it himself, which no living creature has, or the One who owns it has told him, and no pledge exists. With both doors closed, the boast stands in the corridor with nothing behind it, and the following verse begins the accounting.",
  "79": "\"Not at all! We certainly record whatever he claims and will increase his punishment extensively\" gives the verdict on the disbeliever's boast. The previous verse asked whether he had knowledge or a pledge. This verse says no, and describes the consequence.\n\n\"Not at all!\" is a complete rejection. His claim is false. He has no knowledge, no pledge, and no basis for what he says. The very idea that he can predict his standing with God while rejecting His revelations is absurd.\n\nGod records \"whatever he claims.\" Every word he speaks, every boast, every denial is written down. The record does not forget. His arrogance in this life becomes evidence against him in the next.\n\nHis punishment will be \"increased extensively.\" The more he boasts and the more he misleads others, the greater his punishment. A person who sins and then claims that sin is acceptable, or who claims that God will honor him despite his sin, compounds his guilt.\n\nThis verse also warns that words have eternal weight. A careless boast about God, a mocking comment about the Hereafter, and a false claim about salvation are all recorded. The believer should be careful with their tongue, especially when speaking about matters of the unseen.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sober reminder. Do not make careless claims about God or the Hereafter. Every word is being recorded. Speak with humility, and let your hope in God's mercy be expressed through faith and good deeds, not through empty boasting.\n\n'Whatever he claims' covers more than the boast itself. The verse does not say only his blasphemies are written; the claim, the tone, the audience he sought to impress, and the effect on those who heard him are all within the record. Speech about God, the Quran suggests, is never a private matter, and it is never weightless. A person would speak very differently if he could see the pen.",
  "80": "\"And We will inherit what he boasts of, and he will come before Us all by himself\" describes the final end of the disbeliever's wealth and status. The previous verse said his claims are recorded. This verse says that everything he boasted about will be taken.\n\nGod will \"inherit\" what he boasts of. This means that his wealth, his children, and his power will all return to God. He entered the world with nothing, and he will leave it with nothing. The things he treated as permanent are temporary trusts that revert to their true Owner.\n\nThe word \"inherit\" is powerful. An inheritance is what remains after someone has gone. The disbeliever will die, and what remains of his wealth belongs to God. He cannot take it with him. He cannot protect it. He cannot even claim it.\n\nHe will come before God \"all by himself.\" No wealth, no children, no supporters, no tribe. Just him and his deeds. The same is true for every human being. We are born alone, we die alone, and we are resurrected alone.\n\nThis is the ultimate answer to his boast. He claimed he would have wealth and children in the Hereafter, but in the Hereafter he has neither. He has only his disbelief and his record.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of what truly lasts. Do not tie your identity to what you own or who your family is. Those things will not accompany you. What accompanies you is your faith and your deeds. Invest in those, and you will come before God with something worth having.\n\n'He will come before Us all by himself' also reverses the exact items of his boast. He named wealth and children; the verse takes both and leaves the man. The Quran frequently answers a claim point by point, and here the symmetry is almost legal: what he claimed is inventoried, transferred, and the claimant proceeds empty into the presence he mocked.",
  "81": "\"They have taken other gods, instead of Allah, seeking strength and protection through them\" describes the fundamental error of the disbelievers. The previous verses showed that their wealth and children will not save them. This verse explains what they did with their devotion.\n\nInstead of turning to God, they took other gods. The word \"taken\" implies choosing and adopting. They did not merely respect these gods. They gave them the place that belongs to God alone. They hoped for strength and protection from them, the very things that only God can provide.\n\nThe list of things people take as gods is long: idols, angels, saints, money, power, desire, leaders, and even their own opinions. Anything that is given ultimate loyalty, ultimate fear, or ultimate hope becomes a god in that person's life.\n\nThe phrase \"seeking strength and protection through them\" reveals why people do this. They are afraid. They want to feel safe. They look for something tangible, something they can see and control, to protect them. But the things they choose are weaker than they are. The idols cannot hear, the money cannot save them from death, and the power cannot protect them from God.\n\nThis is the same error described throughout the Quran. The people of Noah, Abraham, and Moses all took gods besides God. The mistake is not limited to one time or place. It is the permanent temptation of the human heart to rely on what it can see rather than on the One it cannot see.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a question. What have you taken as a god besides Allah? What do you rely on for strength and protection? If the answer is anything other than God, then you are repeating the error of the disbelievers. True strength and protection belong to Him alone.",
  "82": "\"But no! Those gods will deny their worship and turn against them\" declares the fate of the false gods and those who worshipped them. The previous verse said the disbelievers sought strength through other gods. This verse says those gods will reject them.\n\nThe word \"no\" is a strong rejection of the disbelievers' hopes. The gods they trusted cannot help them. In fact, they will be their opponents on the Day of Judgment. The idols will deny that they ever asked to be worshipped. The prophets, saints, and righteous people who were wrongly worshipped will dissociate themselves from those who did so.\n\nThe Quran tells us that Jesus will say to God on the Day of Judgment that he never told people to worship him or his mother. The angels will deny that they asked to be worshipped. Even Satan will say to his followers that he had no authority over them.\n\nThe phrase \"turn against them\" means that the false gods will be witnesses against their worshippers, not intercessors for them. The very beings the disbelievers hoped would protect them will become part of their condemnation.\n\nThis is one of the most devastating reversals in the Quran. In this life, people defend their idols, fight for them, and sacrifice for them. In the next life, the idols reject them. The people they claimed to love and serve disown them.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning. Do not place your trust in anything that cannot stand with you on the Day of Judgment. The only One who will not abandon you is God. Everything else will either deny you or be destroyed.\n\n'Turn against them' also ends the story of strength the previous verse told. They took these gods seeking power, and the arrangement concludes with the gods standing on the other side of the courtroom. Whatever strength was sought through the created, the Quran's verdict is that the transaction returns the opposite of what was purchased.",
  "83": "\"Do you O Prophet not see that We have sent the devils against the disbelievers, constantly inciting them?\" explains why the disbelievers behave the way they do. The previous verse said the false gods will turn against them. This verse describes the spiritual forces at work in this world.\n\nGod sends the devils against the disbelievers. This does not mean that God forces them to disbelieve. It means that when people choose disbelief, God allows the devils to have influence over them, as a consequence of their choice. The devils do not have power over the faithful servants of God, but they have power over those who turn away.\n\nThe devils \"constantly incite them.\" The word suggests a persistent, ongoing urging. The devils whisper, suggest, tempt, and push. They never tire. They work through desires, doubts, anger, pride, and fear. Their goal is to keep people far from God and deep in sin.\n\nThis is not an excuse for disbelief. The disbelievers are responsible for listening to the devils and acting on their suggestions. But it explains the intensity of their hostility to the truth. They are not merely mistaken. They are being actively pushed away from guidance.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the reality of the spiritual world. The devil is not a metaphor. He is real, and he works tirelessly. The believer is protected from his authority by faith and trust in God, as we learned in Surah an-Nahl. But the one who turns away from God becomes open to his influence.\n\n'Constantly' also removes any picture of occasional temptation. The incitement is described as continuous, which means the believer's safety lies in a continuous counterpart: remembrance, trust, and the refuge the Quran teaches before recitation. A war waged without pauses is met, in the Quran's training, by a vigilance that also does not pause.",
  "84": "\"So do not be in haste against them, for indeed We are closely counting down their days\" consoles the Prophet. The previous verse described the devils inciting the disbelievers. This verse tells him to be patient.\n\nThe Prophet was grieved by the disbelief of his people. He wanted the punishment to come, or at least for them to be guided. He may have felt that the rejection was lasting too long. God tells him not to be in a hurry. His response should be patience, not impatience.\n\nThe phrase \"We are closely counting down their days\" means that every day of their respite is numbered. God is not unaware of what they do. He is keeping a precise count. Their time is running out, and when the end comes, it will not be delayed.\n\nThe countdown is \"closely\" counted, meaning that nothing is missed. Every insult, every act of oppression, every rejection of the truth is recorded. The punishment will come at exactly the right moment, not a moment too soon or a moment too late.\n\nThis is a pattern in the Quran. The Prophet is repeatedly told to be patient and to leave the disbelievers to God. Their respite is not a sign of God's approval. It is a test for them and a trial for the believers.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a lesson in patience. When you see injustice and disbelief, do not rush God's judgment. His timing is perfect. The days of the wrongdoers are being counted. Trust that the One who counts every day will also settle every account.\n\n'Counting down' also reframes what delay feels like from inside. To the impatient, unanswered wrongdoing can read as absence; the verse corrects the ledger without changing the timeline: nothing is being missed, only totalled. A count differs from a clock in one respect, and the difference is the whole message: a clock forgets, a count remembers.",
  "85": "\"Watch for the Day We will gather the righteous before the Most Compassionate as an honoured delegation\" describes the gathering of the believers. The previous verses spoke of the disbelievers and their days being counted. This verse turns to the Day when the righteous are gathered.\n\nThe Day is described with the word \"watch,\" as if the Prophet is being told to look forward to it. It is a day of horror for the disbelievers but a day of honor for the righteous.\n\nThe righteous are gathered \"before the Most Compassionate.\" They are brought into the presence of the God they worshipped and served. Their standing before Him is not the standing of criminals. It is the standing of an \"honoured delegation.\"\n\nThe word for delegation, \"wafd,\" refers to a group that comes to a king as guests or representatives. They are received with honor, given gifts, and treated with generosity. This is how the righteous will be received by God. They will not come as trembling slaves, though they will be humble. They will come as honored guests.\n\nThe use of the name Most Compassionate is significant. The same God whose punishment the disbelievers are fleeing is the God who receives the righteous with mercy. The believers come before His mercy, not before His wrath.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a source of hope. The difficulties of this life will be replaced by an honorable reception in the next. If you are among the righteous, you have something to look forward to that makes every trial of this world small.\n\nThe image of a delegation also implies selection and order. Delegations do not stampede; they are named, gathered, and presented, and each member arrives knowing why he has come. Against the herd driven in the next verse, the picture is of two entirely different modes of arrival, and the mode is decided long before the Day itself.",
  "86": "\"and drive the wicked to Hell like a thirsty herd\" describes the gathering of the disbelievers in contrast to the believers. The previous verse described the righteous as an honored delegation. This verse describes how the wicked are treated.\n\nThe word \"drive\" is harsh. The wicked are not invited or escorted with honor. They are pushed, herded, and forced along. They have no choice and no dignity in how they are brought to judgment.\n\nThey are driven to Hell \"like a thirsty herd.\" A thirsty herd rushes toward water, but in this case, the water they are rushing toward is the Fire. The image reverses what is natural: the herd seeks water to survive, but these people are driven to the very thing that destroys them.\n\nThe word \"thirsty\" also suggests their condition. In this life, they were thirsty for wealth, power, and pleasure but never satisfied. In the next life, that thirst is met with the boiling water of Hell. The desires that drove them in this world become the thing that drives them to punishment.\n\nThe contrast between the two groups could not be sharper. The righteous are an honored delegation before the Most Compassionate. The wicked are a thirsty herd driven to Hell. This is the final division of humanity.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to choose which group you want to be part of. The path of righteousness leads to honor. The path of wickedness leads to being driven like an animal. The choice is made in this life, one day at a time.\n\nThe verse also answers the boast of assembly from earlier in the chapter. The disbelievers asked who was 'superior in assembly,' pointing to their numbers and their gatherings; the final accounting pictures them as the one assembly no one would choose, herded, while the few they mocked are received as guests. The question about crowds receives its answer in the form of two crowds.",
  "87": "\"None will have the right to intercede, except those who have taken a covenant from the Most Compassionate\" describes who can intercede on the Day of Judgment. The previous verses described the two groups. This verse explains who may speak for another.\n\nIntercession, or \"shafa'ah,\" is the act of speaking on behalf of someone else. Many people in this life rely on others to speak for them: lawyers, leaders, family, and friends. On the Day of Judgment, none of that matters. No one can intercede except with God's permission.\n\nThe exception is for those who have \"taken a covenant from the Most Compassionate.\" A covenant is a promise or an agreement. The only people who may intercede are those to whom God has given that right, such as the Prophet Muhammad, the other prophets, the angels, and the righteous. Even then, they may only intercede for those with whom God is pleased.\n\nThe intercession of the Prophet Muhammad is a central belief in Islam. He will intercede for all of humanity on the Day of Standing, and then specifically for the believers. But this intercession does not belong to him independently. It is given to him by God's permission and mercy.\n\nThe verse rejects the idea that idols, saints, or anyone else can intercede without God's permission. The false gods cannot help because they have no covenant with God that allows intercession. They are themselves His creatures, subject to His will.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to rely on God alone for salvation. Hope in the intercession of the Prophet is legitimate, but it must be tied to faith and obedience. No one can guarantee your place in Paradise except through the mercy of the Most Compassionate.\n\n'Taken a covenant' also keeps the exception inside God's hands rather than extending it to the creatures. The right is granted, defined, and limited by the One who judges, and the Quran states elsewhere that intercession belongs only to those to whom He gives permission and whose words He approves. Even the exception, then, is an act of His mercy rather than a loophole in His justice.",
  "88": "\"They say, 'The Most Compassionate has offspring'\" records a claim made by various disbelieving communities. The previous verse spoke of those who take a covenant. This verse returns to those who make a false claim about God.\n\nThe claim that God has offspring has been made by different groups. The pagan Arabs said the angels were daughters of God. Some Jews said Ezra was the son of God. Some Christians said Jesus was the son of God. The Quran rejects all of these claims without distinction.\n\nThe word \"offspring\" includes sons, daughters, and any form of children. To claim that God has a child is to give Him a family, a likeness to created beings, and a partner in His essence. It contradicts His oneness and His self-sufficiency.\n\nThis claim is particularly offensive because it reduces the Creator to the level of His creation. Human beings have children because they need continuation, companionship, and help. God needs none of these. He is the Eternal, the Self-Sufficient, the One who gives life to all but was not given life by anyone.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to purify your belief about God. Do not attribute to Him any of the qualities of created beings. He does not have children, parents, partners, or equals. Any description of God that makes Him like His creation is a distortion of the truth.\n\nThe verse also places the claim in their own mouths, in the present tense. It is not reported as an old error corrected long ago; it is something being said, and the chapter's whole argument is arranged around the sentence. Recording the exact words matters, because the refutation that follows answers the claim as stated, not a softened version of it. The Quran consistently quotes error precisely before correcting it, which is part of why its arguments were heard as fair by friend and enemy alike.",
  "89": "\"You have certainly made an outrageous claim\" responds to the claim of offspring with strong condemnation. The previous verse stated what the disbelievers say. This verse describes the weight of what they have said.\n\nThe word \"outrageous\" translates an Arabic word that means something enormous, monstrous, and almost beyond bearing. The claim that God has a child is not a minor theological difference. It is a monstrous falsehood that shakes the foundations of the heavens and the earth.\n\nThis word is chosen deliberately. The claim is outrageous because of who it is about. It is one thing to make a false claim about a human being. It is another thing to make a false claim about the Creator of the universe. The greater the being, the more serious the falsehood about Him.\n\nThe verse also implies that the claim is outrageous in its consequences. Believing that God has a child leads to worshipping that child, praying to him, and giving him rights that belong to God alone. This is the greatest sin, and it has the most serious consequences.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about the gravity of false belief. Not all errors are equal. Believing something false about God is more serious than believing something false about the world, because the object of the belief is greater. Hold to the truth about God, and reject whatever contradicts it, even if that rejection is unpopular.\n\nThe address also shifts to the second person: 'You have certainly made.' The speaker turns from reporting the claim to facing the claimants, and the weight of the sentence lands on them directly. Direct address of this kind is common in the Quran at moments of maximum seriousness, and the shift of pronoun is itself part of the rhetoric. Listen to how the verse sounds aloud, and the tone is not scholarly disagreement but alarm.",
  "90": "\"by which the heavens are about to burst, the earth to split apart, and the mountains to crumble to pieces\" describes the cosmic reaction to the claim that God has offspring. The previous verse called it an outrageous claim. This verse shows how the universe itself responds.\n\nThe heavens nearly burst, the earth nearly splits, and the mountains nearly crumble. These images convey that the claim is so enormous that creation itself can barely bear to hear it. The order and harmony of the universe are built on the truth that God is one. To deny that is to attack the foundation of all existence.\n\nThe mountains are mentioned because they are the most stable things on earth. If even the mountains are about to crumble, then nothing in creation is unaffected. The most solid structures tremble before false claims about God.\n\nThis is not a literal description of physical events. It is a powerful way of expressing the horror of the claim. In Arabic poetry and Quranic style, the personification of nature reacting to human sin is a way of showing how deeply wrong that sin is. The universe itself is not neutral. It submits to God and trembles at disobedience.\n\nFor the reader, the verse gives a sense of the weight of belief. What you believe about God is not a private matter with no consequences. The whole order of creation is affected by how human beings relate to their Creator. To attribute a child to God is to shake the very foundations of reality.\n\n'About to' is the load-bearing phrase of the verse. The heavens do not burst; they are on the edge of it, held back, and the restraint is itself the point. Creation endures the claim only because its Lord holds it together, and the near-collapse measures the claim precisely: one word more of the universe's own weight, as it were, and the order could not bear it.",
  "91": "\"in protest of attributing children to the Most Compassionate\" explains why the heavens, earth, and mountains react. The previous verse described their reaction. This verse names its cause.\n\nThe reaction is not random. It is a protest. The entire created order is protesting the claim that God has children. The word \"protest\" means that creation is objecting, refusing to accept, and bearing witness against the falsehood.\n\nEvery part of creation knows its Lord. The heavens, the earth, and the mountains worship God and recognize His oneness. When human beings claim that He has a child, they are saying something that every other creature knows to be false. The entire universe is a witness against them.\n\nThe name Most Compassionate is used again, as it has been throughout the chapter. The very God whose mercy encompasses all things is the One they falsely claim has offspring. The name makes the falsehood even more striking, because mercy is one of His attributes, and fatherhood in the created sense implies need and dependency that contradict His mercy and His greatness.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder that you are surrounded by witnesses. The natural world is not on the side of false belief. It proclaims the oneness of God. If you find yourself believing or saying something that the mountains would crumble to hear, stop and reconsider.\n\nThe chapter's most-used name for God also does service here. From its opening prayer to this final passage, the surah has called Him the Most Compassionate again and again, and the claim under protest is now measured against that name. Mercy that covers every creature is being described in the vocabulary of animal need, and the mismatch is what the mountains cannot bear. The protest, in other words, is not only against a falsehood but against an insult to a kindness the whole universe depends on.",
  "92": "\"It does not befit the majesty of the Most Compassionate to have children\" gives the definitive reason why God cannot have offspring. The previous verses described the reaction of creation. This verse states the truth about God.\n\nThe word \"befit\" means that something is suitable, appropriate, or worthy. Having children does not befit God because of who He is. It contradicts His majesty, His self-sufficiency, and His uniqueness.\n\nGod is the Creator of everything. A child, in the created sense, is part of its parents, comes from them, and resembles them. Nothing in existence is part of God, comes from God in that way, or resembles God. He is completely other than His creation.\n\nGod is also the Self-Sufficient. He does not need a child to continue His work, remember His name, or inherit His kingdom. He does not need companionship or help. To say He has a child is to imply a need that He does not have.\n\nThis verse is one of the clearest statements of pure monotheism in the Quran. It does not leave room for compromise. The Most Compassionate does not have children, now or ever, in any form.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to know God as He has described Himself. Do not imagine Him in human terms. Do not give Him the qualities of His creatures. He is greater, higher, and more perfect than anything the human mind can picture.\n\n'To have children' is stated as a permanent impossibility, not a preference. The verse does not say God chooses not to have a son, as a man might choose; it says the thing does not befit Him, the way weight does not befit light. What is being denied is not an act but a compatibility, and that is why the ruling admits no exception and no era. Monotheism in the Quran is argued at exactly this level: not slogans, but what is consistent with who God is.",
  "93": "\"There is none in the heavens or the earth who will not return to the Most Compassionate in full submission\" states the universal return of all creation. The previous verse said God does not befit Him to have children. This verse says every created being returns to Him as a servant.\n\nNo one in the heavens or the earth is exempt. Every angel, every human being, every jinn, and every creature will return to God. The return is \"in full submission,\" meaning as a servant. No one stands beside God as an equal. No one is His child. No one is His partner. All are His servants.\n\nThe word \"return\" implies that this life is temporary. Every created being came from God, and every created being will go back to Him. The arrogant who refuse to worship Him in this life will still return to Him in the next, but their return will be one of humiliation rather than honor.\n\nThe verse is another argument against the claim of offspring. If every being in the heavens and earth returns to God as a servant, then none of them can be His child. A child is not a servant in the same sense. A child shares in the nature of the parent. But all created beings share in the nature of servants, utterly dependent on God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of your true identity. You are a servant of God, whether you accept that role now or not. The only question is whether you will return to Him as a willing, honored servant or as a resistant, humiliated one. Choose to submit now, while the choice is still yours.\n\n'Will not return... in full submission' also levels the hierarchy the claim of offspring tried to build. The angel nearest to God and the quietest human being on earth are brought into the same sentence as servants, and the difference between them is rank within service, not nature. Rank is real in the Quran; sonship is not, and the verse keeps the two firmly apart.",
  "94": "\"Indeed, He fully knows them and has counted them precisely\" affirms God's complete knowledge of every creature. The previous verse said all return to Him in submission. This verse says He knows exactly who they are.\n\n\"Fully knows\" means that God's knowledge of them is complete. He knows their number, their deeds, their secrets, and their futures. Nothing about them is hidden from Him.\n\n\"Counted them precisely\" means that every single one is accounted for. There is no confusion, no estimate, and no forgetting. God does not know His creatures in a general way. He knows each one individually, down to the smallest detail.\n\nThis verse is a comfort for the believer and a warning for the disbeliever. For the believer, it means that God knows your struggles, your efforts, and your situation. You are not a number. You are known. For the disbeliever, it means that every sin and every act of rejection is recorded. You cannot hide.\n\nThe precision of God's count also means that justice will be perfect. No one will be wronged by even the weight of an atom. Every person will receive exactly what they deserve, tempered by God's mercy for those who believed.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to live in the awareness of God's knowledge. You are fully known. Nothing you do is missed. Let that awareness keep you from sin and encourage you in good deeds.\n\nCounting also implies ownership of the kind a shepherd knows his flock, an image the Quran uses elsewhere for what is between God and His servants. The numbers are not statistics; they are a muster of belonging, and every creature answers to it. What the arrogant treat as anonymity, being one among billions, the verse reframes as being individually enrolled. Nobody is lost in the crowd, because there is no crowd, only persons, each known and each numbered.",
  "95": "\"And each of them will return to Him on the Day of Judgment all alone\" repeats the truth of individual return. The previous verse said God has counted every creature. This verse describes how they come back.\n\nEach person returns \"all alone.\" No family, no wealth, no tribe, no nation. Every soul stands before God by itself, with only its deeds. This is one of the most repeated truths in the Quran because it is one of the easiest to forget.\n\nIn this life, people are surrounded by others. They hide in crowds, rely on their connections, and define themselves by their relationships. On the Day of Judgment, all of that falls away. The only thing that remains is the individual and their record.\n\nThe word \"alone\" also emphasizes that no one can bear another's burden. No parent can save their child. No leader can save their followers. Every soul is responsible for itself. The intercession that God permits does not change this fundamental aloneness.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder to prepare for the meeting with God as an individual. Do not rely on your family name, your community, or your teachers. Do your own good deeds. Build your own relationship with God. The day is coming when you will stand before Him with no one else to depend on.\n\n'Each of them' also keeps company with the counting in the previous verse. Having been known one by one and numbered one by one, they return one by one, and the sequence reads like a roll call kept perfectly across centuries. The privacy of that meeting is absolute: no witness is needed, because the One present is the One who counted. Scholars note that this aloneness is also a mercy in disguise. Every pretence a person maintained through life is finally unnecessary in a meeting where nothing can be hidden and nothing needs to be argued; the record speaks, the Judge knows, and the servant stands.",
  "96": "\"As for those who believe and do good, the Most Compassionate will certainly bless them with genuine love\" gives the final promise of the chapter. The previous verses described the return of all people. This verse describes what God gives to the believers.\n\nThe promise is \"genuine love.\" The Arabic word, \"wudd,\" means deep, lasting affection. God will place love for the believers in the hearts of others, and He will love them Himself. This is a special blessing that goes beyond forgiveness and Paradise.\n\nIn this life, the believers who are faithful often have a special place in the hearts of people. Even those who disagree with them may respect their character. More importantly, God loves them. His love is the greatest gift a soul can receive.\n\nThe love is described as genuine because it is not the temporary, conditional love of this world. It is the love of the Most Compassionate, which never fades and never changes. It is the love that leads to being received as an honored delegation on the Day of Judgment.\n\nThe conditions are belief and good deeds, the same two conditions repeated throughout the Quran. Love from God is not earned by birth or lineage. It is given to those who believe and act righteously.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a motivation. Seek God's love above everything else. The love of people is temporary and unreliable. The love of the Most Compassionate is genuine and eternal. Believe and do good, and He will give it to you.\n\nThe Arabic term for the love promised here also appears as a name of God in another chapter, the Loving, which ties the promise to the Giver's own nature. This is not affection won from a reluctant Lord; it flows from who He is, and the chapter has already shown where it leads: to a reception as an honoured delegation. Belief and good deeds do not create this love; they place a person where it is directed.",
  "97": "\"Indeed, We have made this Quran easy in your own language O Prophet so with it you may give good news to the righteous and warn those who are contentious\" explains why the Quran was revealed in Arabic. The previous verse promised love to the believers. This verse describes the purpose of the Book.\n\nThe Quran was made \"easy\" in the Prophet's language. This does not mean it is shallow. It means it is clear, accessible, and revealed in a language the people could understand. The ease is in the clarity of its message, not in the depth of its meaning.\n\nThe purpose of this ease is twofold. First, to give good news to the righteous. They need to hear the promise of God's mercy, Paradise, and love. Good news strengthens their hope and encourages them to keep going.\n\nSecond, to warn those who are \"contentious.\" The word refers to people who argue, dispute, and oppose the truth. They need to hear the warning of punishment. The same Quran that gives good news to some gives warning to others, depending on the condition of their hearts.\n\nThe fact that the Quran was revealed in the Prophet's language is also a reminder that every messenger was sent in the language of his people. God did not send a book in a language no one could understand. He sent it in clear human language so that people would have no excuse.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to benefit from the ease of the Quran. It has been made easy to understand, so read it, reflect on it, and act on it. Do not make it harder than it is. Let its good news give you hope, and let its warnings keep you from arrogance.\n\nThe pairing of good news and warning also describes the Book's whole temperament. It never flatters and never terrorizes; it informs, and lets each heart take from the message what its condition allows. The same recitation that settles the righteous has unsettled the stubborn since it was first recited, and the ease of the language has never been the variable that decides which response it receives.",
  "98": "\"Imagine how many peoples We have destroyed before them! Do you still see any of them, or even hear from them the slightest sound?\" concludes the chapter with a final warning. The previous verse explained the purpose of the Quran. This verse points to the fate of earlier nations.\n\nThe command to \"imagine\" invites reflection on the ruins of history. People who once lived, built, argued, and rejected their prophets are gone. Their cities are empty, their power is forgotten, and their voices are silent. The same fate awaits those who follow their path.\n\nThe two questions make the point vivid. Do you see any of them? Their bodies are gone, their buildings are in ruins, and their civilization has vanished. Do you hear even the slightest sound? There is no whisper of their former greatness. The noise of their markets, their armies, and their celebrations is completely silent.\n\nThis is not the first time the chapter has pointed to destroyed nations. It began with stories of prophets who were rejected, and it ends with the silence of those who rejected them. The message is consistent: disbelief has a terminus. No matter how powerful a people become, they cannot outlast God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a final warning and a final comfort. It warns that the way of the disbelievers ends in silence. It comforts the believers that the oppressors will not last forever. The Quran that was made easy for you is your guide. Hold fast to it, believe, do good, and you will be among the righteous who receive God's genuine love.\n\nThe silence named at the end is also exact. The verse does not say their descendants are gone or their ideas are gone; it says no sight of them and no sound from them remains, a description of total removal from witness. A people who filled their era with noise are evoked by the one thing they cannot supply, which is a whisper."
};
